}\uc1\pard\lang1033\ul\f0\fs24 1.00\tab Introduction\ulnone\f1\par
\par
The \ul\i P?S\super\f0  \nosupersub\f1 Enhanced PDP-8 Simulator\ulnone\i0  (\i PEPS\i0 ) is a software\f0 -based simulation\f1   of \f0 a\f1  12-bit\f0  \ul\i Digital Equipment Corporation\ulnone\i0  (\i DEC\i0 ) \ul\i\f1 PDP-8\ulnone\i0\f0  series \f1 computer\f0 .  The package includes support for many\f1  of \f0 the \f1 peripheral\f0  devices found within real-world hardware configurations; certain PEPS package components were created by various members of the \ul\i P?S\ulnone\super\i0 [1]\nosupersub  on the original hardware.\par
\par
\f1 The overall simulat\f0 ed environment\f1  is\f0  determined\f1  by specific initialization and\f0  \f1 configuration files \f0 controlling \f1 the basic "engine" of the \f0 PEPS package: the \ul\i\f1 SIMH\ulnone\super\i0\f0 [2]\nosupersub\f1  \f0 simulator \f1 for\f0  the DEC \ul\i\f1 PDP-8\f0 /E\ulnone\i0  and similar computers\f1 .\par
\par
\f0 This document serves as the primary user guide to all features of the PEPS package.  Unless otherwise indicated, default PEPS configuration files are assumed to be in effect; this enables all PDP-8 devices\super [3]\nosupersub  required for either entry-level (paper-tape-oriented) programs or any of several provided single-user storage-based operating systems as described below in section 1.10.\par
\par
\f1 While the \f0 SIMH simulator \f1 can be used to \f0 create\f1  \f0 a \f1 minimal\f0  paper-tape oriented\f1  \f0 environment\f1 , emphasis is given to larger systems that\f0  can support any of several included single-user storage-based operating systems.  This allows execution of more complex programs, some of which are specific to the operating system environment chosen.\par
\par
If desired, new programs can be created using a variety of development techniques.  In the PEPS environment, program development focuses on using facilities available on the host \ul\i Microsoft Windows\ulnone\i0  platform.  Depending on the user's needs, this can include creation of output files meant to be viewed in the host environment; in specific cases, various forms of post-processing can be applied for the purpose of creating output suitable for printing on Windows-compliant printers or viewing on-screen with commonly available document-processing programs (such as \ul\i Adobe Reader\ulnone\i0 ).  PEPS package usage has included development of PDP-8 assembly language listings of well-organized source code; in specific instances this has produced fully-formatted listing files of programs previously unavailable (other than in the primitive form of badly copied paper listings that limits any meaningful usage).\par
\par
\f1 This \f0 u\f1 ser \f0 g\f1 uide, together with the documents referenced in\f0  various appendices\f1 , provides information on the installation, maintenance and operation of the\f0  \f1 PEPS\f0  package\f1 .\par
\par
\f0 System requirements and initial installation.\f1\par
\par
The PEPS package\f0  \f1 is designed to work on any release\super\f0 [4]\nosupersub\f1  of Microsoft \ul\i Windows XP\ulnone\i0 , \ul\i Windows Vista\ulnone\i0 , \ul\i Windows 7\ulnone\i0 , \ul\i Windows 8\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Windows 10\ulnone\i0 .  It is generally distributed as a self-extracting archive capable of being installed on any permanently lettered logical drive.  Due to internal restrictions, the PEPS \f0 package \f1 cannot be located on network shares or removable drives where the logical drive letter \f0 is subject to\f1  change.\page Appendix \f0 A\f1  de\f0 scribes\f1  the installation proce\f0 dure, including the ability to create custom descendent installation packages should the user wish to include additional files or PEPS features beyond the standard release\f1 .\f0   All details of the installation process must be closely followed in order to achieve a usable result.\par
\par
Logical drive considerations.\f1\par
\par
\f0 While not widely known, t\f1 he use of multiple \f0 logical \f1 drives in Windows is\f0  actually\f1  encouraged by Microsoft.  This is best accomplished using \f0 various\f1  third-party \f0 hard drive \f1 partitioning programs; however, certain Windows releases include \ul\i Administrative Tools\ulnone\i0  capable of \f0 performing a rudimentary form of partitioning that may be adequate for some users.\par
\par
Appendix C describes several third-party partitioning programs that are compatible with all operating systems that support the PEPS package.  These utilities also include additional features that may be of use to advanced users beyond the scope of logical drive placement of the PEPS package.\par
\par
\f1 Note: For illustrat\f0 ive\f1  purposes only, the PEPS package elements will be described throughout this document as if installed on \f0 Windows logical \f1 drive \ul\i P\f0 :\ulnone\i0\f1 .  Any permanently lettered logical device may be used\f0 ;\f1  \f0 h\f1 owever, it is recommended \f0 the \f1 PEPS\f0  package\f1  be installed on a dedicated drive to facilitate backup of working copies of the package.\par
\par
\f0 Additional required utilities not supplied with the PEPS package.\f1\par
\par
Certain auxiliary programs compatible with Windows are \f0 likely\f1  required\f0  depending on the user's needs; Appendix C contains descriptions of many of these additional applications.  Among the utilities presented are\f1  various text editors\f0  known to be compatible with the PEPS package\f1  and post-processing programs to facilitate viewing or printing text files created by PEPS \f0 (\f1 such as assembly listings of programs\f0  under development)\f1 .\par
\par
Most of the PEPS documentation files\f0  (including this user guide)\f1  are distributed in \ul\i Portable Document Format\ulnone\i0  \f0 (\i\f1 PDF\i0\f0 ).  \f1 As such, a compatible document\f0 -processing\f1  program \f0 (\f1 such as \ul\i Adobe Reader\ulnone\i0\f0 )\f1  \f0 must be installed prior to the full usage of the PEPS package\f1 .\par
\par
\f0 The rest of this document assumes the PEPS package and all necessary third-party applications are already installed and ready to use; as the user gains familiarity with the package, minor adjustments may be implemented as necessary.  In particular, calls to the installed PDF document reader must be updated in certain BATCH script files to reflect the current installation; calls to other utilities must be updated in an analogous manner where relevant.  Appendix C contains more information on these utilities and the interaction with the associated script files.  Examples will be provided for calling common programs from within both 32-bit and 64-bit operating systems. \par
\par
Note: It is recommended that all relevant considerations described above be reconciled and fully understood before proceeding. \page\ul\f1 1.1\f0 0\tab\f1 General Description\ulnone\par
\par
The PEPS package provides a functional simulation of a PDP-8 system with\f0  an array of available\f1  peripherals.  When compared to real-world hardware, the \f0 included \f1 configuration\f0 s\f1  \f0 are\f1  considered \f0 to span the range from entry-level through \f1 large\f0 -scale\f1  system\f0 s.  The package \f1 provides much of the performance of original PDP-8 systems while actually running on a Windows-based personal computer\f0  through software simulation\f1 .\par
\par
As typically configured, the following \f0 PDP-8\f1  resources are generally available within the PEPS environment:\line\line a) \ul\i PDP-8/E\ulnone\i0  \f0 Central Processing Unit (\i CPU\i0 )\par
\par
   Note: The\f1  \i KK8F\i0\f0  CPU i\f1 s \f0 used\f1  in\f0  several DEC models such as \f1 the\par
\f0    \ul\i\f1 PDP-8/E\ulnone\i0 ,\f0  \ul\i\f1 PDP-8/F\ulnone\i0 , \ul\i PDP-8/M\ulnone\i0  and\f0  \ul\i\f1 PDP-8/A-620\ulnone\i0\f0  \f1 series\f0  models; no\par
   software method can determine which of these models is in use.\par
   Common practice is to refer to the \ul\i PDP-8/E\ulnone\i0  since this was the first\par
   model produced including this hardware.\par
\par
b\f1 ) \ul\i\f0 KE8E\ulnone\i0  e\f1 xtended \f0 a\f1 rithmetic \f0 e\f1 lement \f0 (\i\f1 EAE\i0\f0 )\f1\par
\par
\f0    Note: with\f1  \f0 the EAE option installed, an improvement in model\par
   identification occurs.  If a machine is determined to be a PDP-8/A,\par
   it could be referred to as the \ul\i PDP-8/A-625\ulnone\i0 ; however, as described\par
   above, common practice still refers to the PDP-8/E (with EAE).\par
\f1\par
\f0 c\f1 ) 32K of main memory\f0\par
\par
   The SIMH simulator supports commands to deliberately limit memory\par
   size to smaller amounts by 4K field increments (analogous to the\par
   manner in which real-world machines can be configured).  (Note:\par
   Certain supported operating systems include commands to\par
   deliberately ignore available memory.  This is occasionally used\par
   to protect the contents of the upper portions of memory in specific\par
   circumstances.\par
\par
   Many system-specific programs need to be tested on smaller\par
   configurations to confirm proper operation of dynamic allocation\par
   routines for all supported instances of memory size.  For example,\par
   the various PAL language assemblers require additional memory to\par
   assemble larger programs as a function of symbol count.  Developing\par
   such programs requires that all supported memory sizes function as\par
   intended.  Each supported operating system accomplishes memory size\par
   adjustment by system-specific commands; consult the appropriate\par
   software manuals for further details.\par
\par
   There is some overlapping of memory size limitations between the\par
   SIMH simulator initialization files and the internal features of\par
   the various operating systems; however, changing the initialization\par
   files for SIMH then requires initiating a new SIMH session.  The\par
   changes brought about by the system-specific commands can generally\par
   be accomplished either immediately or by at most a restart of the\par
   operating system.  There are situations where it is useful to boot\par
   to more than one operating system within a single SIMH session,\par
   perhaps to perform inter-system file conversions.\page    Note: By adjusting certain parameters within the configuration\par
   files, the availability of EAE, memory size and other particulars\par
   may be changed.  Appendix Q contains more information on related\par
   SIMH parameters which may be of interest to advanced users.\par
\f1\par
d) \f0 System c\f1 onsole terminal\f0  display device (device 03/04)\par
\par
   While original real-world PDP-8 systems generally used the \ul\i Teletype\ulnone\i0\par
   \ul\i Model 33\ulnone\i0  \ul Automatic Send and Receive\ulnone  (\i ASR\i0 ) terminal as the system\par
   console, this implementation is fully compliant with more recent\par
   devices (such as the \ul\i VT05\ulnone\i0  display terminal) with regard to handling\par
   \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  characters visually aligned to multiples of eight spaces.\par
   Depending on operating system specifics, console terminal output\par
   may use \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  as described here, or simulate the display using a\par
   calculated number of \cf1\highlight2 <SP>\cf0\highlight0  characters.\f1\par
\par
\f0    I\f1 n \f0 many\f1  \f0 a\f1 spects\f0  \f1 the console\f0  \f1 display resembles the output\f0  \f1 of a\par
\f0   \f1  Teletype Model 33\f0  printing unit\f1  using pale\f0  \f1 yellow roll paper and\par
\f0    \f1 bold black\f0  \f1 ink\f0 ; this represents a compromise between the original\par
   teletype output appearance and display devices found on newer\par
   systems well after paper-tape software became obsolescent.\f1\par
\par
   Note: For operating systems\f0  \f1 such as \ul\i OS/8\ulnone\super\i0 [\f0 5\f1 ]\nosupersub\f0  \f1 with known defects in\par
\f0    \f1 console terminal\f0  \f1 handling, the console simulation \f0 will\f1  be\par
\f0    \f1 restricted\f0  to \f1 forc\f0 ed\f1  upper-case input and output, as well as\par
\f0    \f1 suppression of\f0  superfluous\f1  output of certain control characters\par
\f0    which are known to have visual representation in the host Windows\par
   environment\f1 .\par
\par
e) \f0 System lineprinter (device 66)\par
\par
   Standard PDP-8 lineprinters (such as the \ul\i LP08\ulnone\i0 ) print on fan-fold\par
   paper up to ANSI B (\i Ledger\i0 /\i Tabloid\i0 ) size of 11" x 17".  This allows\par
   66 lines and up to 132 columns per page.  This device is generally\par
   referred to as \ul\i LPT:\ulnone\i0 .  Using post-processing techniques available\par
   within the PEPS package, LPT: output can be created in PDF format. \par
\par
   Note: For certain configurations, the lineprinter device will be\par
   deliberately disabled.  If this is the case, attempting to output\par
   to device 66 will not raise the done flag leading to device\par
   time-out.\par
\par
\f1 f) \ul\i PC04\ulnone\i0  \f0 h\f1 igh-\f0 s\f1 peed \f0 r\f1 eader\f0 /punch\f1  \f0 (\f1 device 01\f0 /02)\par
\par
   The high-speed reader is generally referred to as the \ul\i PTR:\ulnone\i0  device.\par
   All high-speed reader input is setup in the host environment for\par
   the next SIMH session; after all character frames have been read,\par
   further attempts to obtain input from the PTR: device will result\par
   in device time-out.\par
\par
   The high-speed punch is generally referred to as the \i PTP:\i0  device.\par
   High-speed punch output is cumulative across SIMH sessions.\par
\par
   Note: Individual file transfer from the PEPS host environment to\par
   the SIMH environment is achieved by using the PTR: device.  File\par
   transfer from the SIMH environment to the PEPS host environment is\par
   achieved by using either the PTP: device or the LPT: device.\page\f1 g) \ul\i TC08\ulnone\i0  \ul\i DECtape\ulnone\i0  \f0 c\f1 ontroller\f0  (device 76-77)\par
\par
   The TC08 DECtape controller can be configured with up to eight\par
   DECtape drive units numbered 0 through 7.  Each logical drive is\par
   configured by association with an active compatible device file\par
   which represents the contents of a specified DECtape drive.  If the\par
   associated drive file does not exist, it will be created\par
   automatically when attempting to access the logical drive unit.\par
\par
   The TC08 DECtape controller is based on the TC01 DECtape\par
   controller.  While there are real-world differences between the\par
   two devices, all standard TC08 DECtape software runs on either\par
   device.\par
\par
   Note: Attempting access to a non-configured logical drive will\par
   cause a \ul\i SELECT\ulnone\i0  error within the TC08 controller; each supported\par
   operating system is responsible for handling all errors.  Certain\par
   interrupt-driven application programs will not function properly if\par
   this condition occurs and the associated error flag is not cleared.\par
   Should this situation become a problem, configuring all logical\par
   units to be associated with active drive files will remedy the\par
   situation.  (Default configuration files supplied with the PEPS\par
   release properly configure all logical drives for the TC08 and\par
   other devices.  Advanced users can easily modify the configuration\par
   files to add support for custom device files as required)\par
 \par
\f1 h) Primary\f0  \ul\i\f1 TD8E\ulnone\i0  \f0 c\f1 ontroller\f0  (\f1 device 77\f0 )\par
\par
   The TD8E DECtape controller can be configured \f1 with up to two\par
 \f0   \f1 DECtape drive\f0  units numbered 0 through 1 \f1 with the same file\par
\f0  \f1  \f0  \f1 considerations\f0  for the TC08 controller\f1  as\f0  described above\f1  in\f0  \f1 g).\par
\par
\f0    \f1 The\f0  primary\f1  TD8E\f0  \f1 controller \f0 conflicts with \f1 the TC08\f0  \f1 controller\f0  due\par
   to overlapping device codes; as such they are \f1 mutually exclusive.\par
\f0    Three additional TD8E Controllers are defined as follows:\par
\par
   \i i\i0 .   The secondary TD8E controller (\f1 device 7\f0 6) can be configured\par
        with up to two DECtape drive units numbered 2 through 3 with\par
        the same file considerations for the TC08 controller as\par
        described above in g).\par
\par
        The secondary TD8E controller conflicts with the TC08\par
        controller due to overlapping device codes; as such, they are\par
        mutually exclusive.\par
\par
\i    ii\i0 .  The tertiary TD8E controller (\f1 device 7\f0 5) can be configured\par
        with up to two DECtape drive units numbered 4 through 5 with\par
        the same file considerations for the TC08 controller as\par
        described above in g).\par
\par
        The tertiary TD8E controller conflicts with the primary RX8E\par
        controller described below due to overlapping device codes; as\par
        such, they are mutually exclusive.\page\i    iii\i0 . The quaternary TD8E controller (device 74) can be configured\par
        with up to two DECtape drive units numbered 6 through 7 with\par
        the same file considerations for the TC08 controller as\par
        described above in g).\par
\par
        The quaternary TD8E controller conflicts with the RK8E\par
        controller described below due to overlapping device codes; as\par
        such, they are mutually exclusive.\par
\par
   Note: At the present time, the SIMH simulator only supports one of\par
   the TD8E controllers in any given configuration.  As such, certain\par
   specific TD8E configurations available on real-world systems cannot\par
   be realized on the SIMH simulator.\par
\par
   By design, the SIMH simulator cannot simultaneously support\par
   the TC08 controller and any TD8E controller despite the fact the\par
   tertiary and quaternary TD8E controllers are not in device code\par
   conflict with the TC08 controller.  Pertinent real-world systems\par
   are generally configured in an analogous matter; as such, this is\par
   not a serious consideration.  Additionally, such configurations are\par
   in conflict with either the \ul\i RX8E\ulnone\i0  controller or the \ul\i RK8E\ulnone\i0  controller\par
   as described below.\par
\par
\f1 i) \ul\i RK8E\ulnone\i0  \f0 removable c\f1 artridge \f0 d\f1 isk \f0 c\f1 ontroller\f0  (\f1 device 74\f0 )\par
\par
   The \f1 RK8E\f0  removable c\f1 artridge \f0 d\f1 isk\f0  controller can be configured with\par
   up to four \ul\i RK05\ulnone\i0  drive units numbered 0 through 3.  \f1 As in the case\par
 \f0   \f1 of\f0  \f1 DECtape drives, should the associated\f0  drive file not exist, it\par
   will be created automatically when first accessed.\par
\par
   Note: A modified controller known as the \ul\i RKS8E\ulnone\i0  allows up to eight\par
   RK05 drives.  However, this device is not supported by SIMH at this\par
   time.  Each supported operating system must be configured to avoid\par
   anomaly with regard to misinterpreting the actual configuration.\par
\par
j) Primary \ul\i RX8E\ulnone\i0  controller (device 75)\par
\par
   The RX8E controller can be configured with up to two \ul\i RX01\ulnone\i0  drive\par
   units numbered 0 through 1.  As in the case of RK05 or DECtape\par
   drives, should the associated drive file not exist, it will be\par
   created automatically when first accessed.\par
\par
   A secondary RX8E controller (device 76) is defined; however, device\par
   conflicts exist with both the TC08 DECtape controller and the\par
   secondary TD8E controller.  As such, most configurations do not\par
   support this device.\par
\par
   Note: Various real-world systems (such as the \ul\i VT78\ulnone\i0 ) support an\par
   extended primary RX8E-compatible controller that can be configured\par
   with an additional \ul\i RX01\ulnone\i0 /R\ul\i X02\ulnone\i0  drive pair.  The SIMH simulator does\par
   not currently support these device extensions.\par
\par
Additional devices may be available depending on enabling specific configuration parameters of the SIMH simulator.  Additionally, certain devices are in direct conflict with each other, thus judicious choices must be made.  Appendix D includes a list of these devices; however, specific software drivers and/or additional utility programs may be required for proper support.\par
\page Note: Limitations of the SIMH environment prevent dismountable devices (such as DECtapes, RK05 and RX01 drives) from being loaded or unloaded during SIMH sessions\par
\par
One of the design goals of the \f1 PEPS package\f0  is to allow advanced users the ability to modify the current configuration as easily as possible. However, the SIMH simulator must be restarted after any changes are made to the configuration files.\par
\par
Wherever possible, the various components of the SIMH simulator are maintained in separate directories to avoid clutter and needless confusion.  Emphasis is given to accessing configuration files that determine the exact simulation during any SIMH session.\par
\par
Any number of configurations can be made available in a PEPS installation; however, only one configuration can be run at any given time.  This allows maximum flexibility in terms of mounted device specifics and operating system configurations easily accessible from a single directory.\par
\par
\ul 1.20\tab General Usage\ulnone\par
\par
Note: PDP-8 software supports several different file structures, all referred to in the SIMH simulator environment as \ul\i directories\ulnone\i0 .  In Windows, the analogous structures are often referred to as \ul\i folders\ulnone\i0 .  Within this user guide, the terms will be used interchangeably only when referring to the file structures of Microsoft Windows.\par
\par
The user must have a general understanding of Microsoft Windows to make use of many of the available features of the \f1 PEPS package\f0 .  This includes the ability to navigate \i Explorer\i0  windows for the purpose of file manipulation; this generally consists of cutting or copying and pasting of files between Explorer windows, file deletion and in-place file renaming using either keyboard commands or mouse clicks as a matter of preference.  Any of the various \ul\i Windows for Dummies\ulnone\i0  and related self-help manuals widely available may be consulted as required.\par
\par
Using the PEPS package typically starts by opening the PEPS \ul\i Command\ulnone\i0  directory window on the Windows desktop; as the name implies, this serves as the "central command" for most PEPS operations.  From this open directory, nearly all PEPS functions and utilities can be initiated.  This includes bringing up the SIMH PDP-8 simulator booting to one of several supported operating system configurations, and various file-related functions such as file editing and/or file maintenance in the host Windows environment.  The Command directory is detailed in section 1.50 below.\par
\par
While emphasis is made within the PEPS package to working with storage-based operating systems, it is possible to recreate the tedious environment of the original PDP-8 models that had few peripherals.  The original systems had only a console device, often a Teletype Model 33 ASR over and above the basic CPU and memory.  When using a PDP-8 with such meager hardware, the only realistic way to approach the system is by the use of paper-tapes, both in binary and text format.  A useful option is the high-speed reader and punch used to make the development process somewhat faster.  Several years after the original models were introduced, storage peripherals were made available; most of the functionality of the PEPS package is concerned with the usage of operating systems and other software for storage devices.\par
\par
The use of the so-called \ul\i Paper-tape Operating System\ulnone\i0  is described below and also in section 1.41.  Appendix F includes details of some working sessions that can be used as examples for additional paper-tape system program usage.\par
\par
For use of the PDP-8 with defined storage devices, it is essential to have some working knowledge of these systems and the facilities they provide.  It may be desirable to use multiple operating systems to take advantage of unique features and/or draw comparisons between the supported system.\par
\par
Appendix B includes references to documents for the various supported PDP-8 storage-based operating systems which can aid in their usage. Many of these documents are provided only in the full PEPS distribution which will not be widely distributed in part due to sheer size (although certain manuals may be available from various online file archives).\par
\par
More comprehensive releases are intended for developers of either the PEPS package itself or any of the supported operating systems, especially P?S/8.  Any questions regarding specific extensions to the PEPS package beyond the public release are encouraged to contact the author, Charles Lasner, who is also the principal architect of P?S/8.  This would include obtaining information about additional documents which may be available beyond the basic PEPS release.\par
\par
Paper-tape system usage and limitations.\par
\par
While program development and execution can be accomplished in the limited environment of the \ul Paper-tape Operating System\ulnone , there are many steps to operating the computer in such direct terms.  The components of the \ul Paper-tape Operating System\ulnone  are binary programs that must be loaded into memory and then started in various ways.  Often, the exact functionality is determined by the setting of the front panel switches when the program is (re)started.  Commands exist in the basic SIMH implementation to examine and deposit values to memory locations or the basic registers of the computer (the Accumulator, Link and Multiplier Quotient) and the front panel switches.  As shown in Appendix F, the PAL III assembler requires several passes over the ASCII text input tape, each with a different front panel setting.  The exact value is determined by specific pass and user intention regarding certain options controlled by designated front panel switch bits.  The input tape must be reset at the beginning of each pass by assigning a designated file to the PTR device.\line\line As such, using the paper-tape environment requires a large amount of "hands-on" operations, albeit simulated by commands.  This represents a faithful portrayal of the difficulty using the original computer hardware.\par
\par
Further information regarding specific commands and settings is available in section 1.41 and Appendix F below.\par
\par
