\pard          Figure 2.  P?S/8 System Status and Available Resources\page Further information on using the supported operating systems is provided in section 1.61 below.\par
\par
Future releases of the PEPS package may support additional storage-based operating systems; the initial release only supports \ul\i OS/8\ulnone\i0  and\par
\ul\i P?S/8\ulnone\i0  in various configurations; these are the two systems deemed most useful for typical PDP-8 usage.  In specific instances, it may prove to be difficult to support other systems without the relevant source code files; the files may be difficult to obtain and/or were intended to be developed on other hardware systems such as the \ul\i PDP-10\ulnone\i0  \ul\i TOPS10\ulnone\i0  operating system.  Those desiring to support these systems are encouraged to gain proficiency with both OS/8 and P?S/8 program development features, since a combination of utilities available in both systems may be required to allow proper support entirely based within the PEPS environment.  In general, the P?S/8 \ul\i PAL\ulnone\i0  assembler is a superset of OS/8 \ul\i PAL8\ulnone\i0  and supports nearly all TOPS10 \ul\i PAL10\ulnone\i0  features that might apply.\par
\par
Note: It is entirely possible to develop and execute certain PDP-8 programs from virtually all available storage-based operating systems merely by adherence to the prevailing memory conventions.  However, each operating system supports specific unique features that can require complete program conformance for proper operation in all configurations, some of which do not apply to the PEPS package.  This will be discussed in greater detail in section 1.61 below.  Appendix R contains documentation of all important considerations with regard to program conformance with the various operating systems.\par
\par
\ul 1.40\tab Using the SIMH simulator for program development\ulnone\par
\par
While it is possible to execute existing PDP-8 programs within the SIMH environment, the full implementation (including the PEPS extensions) allows for meaningful development of any PDP-8 program; in many cases, work flow in the PEPS environment exceeds that of the original hardware configuration.  Various operating modes are described below.\par
\par
\ul 1.41\tab Using the SIMH simulator for basic PDP-8 programming\ulnone\par
\par
The SIMH PDP-8 simulator has been developed over the course of many years, gradually adding in numerous features and functionality beyond the original implementation.  Earlier releases generally concentrated on mimicking the most primitive PDP-8 systems, which only had a limited capability generally referred to (somewhat euphemistically) as the\i  \ul Paper-tape operating system\ulnone\i0 .\par
\par
In actuality, this is merely a series of inter-related binary files stored on BIN\super [7] \nosupersub format binary paper-tapes intended to be loaded into memory from either the high-speed reader or the low-speed reader attached to either the Teletype Model 33 ASR or Model 35 ASR terminal.\par
\par
Software commands were then added to the SIMH simulator as a stand-in for a hardware front panel; at that point, the scope of what could be accomplished was limited to what could be done with the \i paper-tape operating system\i0  components and any additional binary paper-tapes created by the user.  This situation can best be described as a tedious waste of development time due to the frustrating changes of contents of memory carrying out each of the many steps; each step would take significant time and effort.  The programmer's thought process regarding the details of the actual program being developed was often interrupted by the overhead of moving onto the next small step in a very long chain of events that accomplished very little.\par
\par
The issues associated with the \ul\i paper-tape operating system\ulnone\i0  are actually intensified when using the SIMH simulator as there is maximum dependency on the internal commands added in lieu of a hardware front panel.  Each component program requires the use of the paper-tape BIN loader to have it loaded into memory, followed by inspection of the accumulator register (AC) when the loader halts to ensure proper loading of the latest utility.  Assuming no errors in loading, the latest component must be started at a designated address with a different manual operation.\page Generally, the front panel switches must be set to specific values to inform the program of the general nature of the current usage of the program; this in turn could lead to several passes in the case of an assembler program (generally the primitive \ul\i PAL III\ulnone\i0  assembler) with the CPU halting after each pass over an input source file tape.\par
\par
Paper-tape editing programs must also be loaded and started; during the editing session, the paper-tape source file must be read in and also a descendant copy punched.  Assuming a nominal successful assembly in several passes leading to a punched binary paper-tape of the program being developed, this program binary paper-tape must also be loaded by the BIN loader, etc.  Each context change of memory requires several commands entered into the running SIMH session to either monitor or carry out relatively small operations at the expense of maximum programmer/operator effort and potential for tedium.\line  \par
While this primitive capability still remains within the SIMH simulator, the addition of a large array of storage and other peripherals allows meaningful usage of more complex systems.  Software operating systems that are booted up from the supported devices allows any level of user to accomplish much more than just a basic system (whether real-world based or simulated).  In typical real-world systems, program development that using a storage-based operating system could be accomplished in perhaps a matter of hours could take months to accomplish when limited to the \ul\i paper-tape operating system\i0  \ulnone components.\par
\line For those who only want to use this limited capability, two alternatives are possible: a) The tedious process as described above involving constantly changing the SIMH configuration at each step, b) Using a few commands from the supported operating systems to eliminate several of the steps requiring more intense management.  Minimal\par
knowledge of the supported operating systems allows use of the basic software without experiencing some portion of the associated tedium. If the simulated environment merely consists of a CPU with memory and a console terminal (generally a Teletype Model 33 ASR) and perhaps a PC04 high-speed reader and punch, this predictably leads to user frustration as is also the case on a real-world equivalent hardware system.\par
\par
Both the P?S/8 and OS/8 operating systems contain commands to support the rudiments of what can aid in supporting this primitive software environment.  Appendix F details the specific programs used to support paper-tape operations available in both storage-based operating systems; only a small number of easily-learned commands are required to fully support improving the limited capability of the paper-tape environment.\par
\par
While the specific programs used in the paper-tape environment are not provided with the PEPS package, they are available from various Internet-based PDP-8 archive libraries.  Due to the nature of the overall PEPS environment, these programs may be added to allow easy deployment as required; some additional knowledge of the storage-based operating systems may be required.  In general, the storage-based operating systems provide superior equivalents to these programs; as such, these earlier programs are not included in the PEPS package.\page\ul 1.42\tab Using the full PEPS environment for PDP-8 programming\ulnone\line\line As compared to the paper-tape environment, either of the two provided operating systems (OS/8 and P?S/8) can be called up to perform far more sophisticated operations including running additional programming languages, file editors and other utilities unique to each operating system.  Each system is capable of self-contained independent operation including program development.  Commands within the PEPS Command directory are available to initiate these operations, as well as transmit files from the host Windows environment to the SIMH environment or vice-versa.\par
\par
The two operating systems can also exchange the contents of files using available conversion programs, most notably the P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  utility used for interchanging text files.\par
\par
Binary files can be transferred between the two operating systems using various methods including binary paper-tape punching in one system followed by binary file reading in the other.  The Command directory includes a shortcut/icon to an internal routine to swap the \i PTRIN\i0  and \i PTPOUT\i0  files for this specific purpose.  Note: Future releases of the P?S/8 operating system will include an additional utility to be known as \i BINCON\i0  patterned after OS8CON to more directly effect binary data transfers between the two operating systems.  This will allow OS/8 users the ability to more readily utilize the binary output of the superior P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  assembler program.  PAL includes features not available within OS/8's \i PAL8\i0  utility including additional language features, advanced support of the LINC-8 and PDP-12 LINC processor and corrected support of the DTORG directive to allow meaningful usage of this feature in both operating systems.\par
\par
Configurations of the two operating systems are each capable of booting up the other from within the same SIMH session.  Appendix G documents the available methods to transfer files between operating systems (some of which are currently under development as of this writing).  By using the best features of both systems, the PEPS package can accommodate any level of usage and development realizable in a PDP-8-based environment.\par
\par
Appendix F illustrates a minimal paper-tape session for those who want to experience this minimal usage of the PEPS package.  Some of the paper-tape software programs are being added to the PEPS package in a dedicated folder for the purpose.  The system manifest file should be checked for a current list of included files.\par
\par
\ul 1.43\tab Host editing and development of PDP-8 source files\ulnone\par
\par
While the prevailing operating systems are capable of independent source file development, a distinctive feature of the PEPS environment is the support of host-level file editing with rapid transmission of updated files into the SIMH environment; multiple rounds of editing sessions and program assembly or execution (in the case of high-level languages such as FOCAL) can quickly be achieved.  While the standard Windows Notepad editor is the default editing program for the supplied editing commands, any completely conforming alternative editing program can be used.  Appendix D contains information on some of these editing programs and certain tradeoffs involved when using them.\par
\par
There are two basic approaches to performing host editing and development of source files:\page 1) Easily specify a unique filename limited to a generic directory using pre-existing generic commands; only one designated file at a time can be used with this method.\par
\par
The generic \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory can contain any number of unique filenames; selecting one for development allows access to a set of available commands already included in the package. \par
\par
This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the Current_Program_Files directory such as \ul Set Current\i  \i0 Program\ulnone  and \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone .\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
An additional feature of the PEPS package is to readily work with any one file in the generic \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  The only limitation is that access is limited to the currently designated file as the commands are generic to all such files.  This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the Current_Program_Files directory such as \ul Set Current\i  \i0 Program\ulnone  and \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone .\par
\par
\par
2) Use a pair of custom commands specific to a designated project file; any number of unique command pairs for specific files can be defined.\par
\par
As described in 2) above, for any programming project based on a particular file, two icon-based command shortcuts are required specific to that file.  Any number of such projects can be defined and be in different stages of development at the same time.\page It is recommended only advanced users attempt to create their own command pairs. The process requires significant knowledge of arcane features of Microsoft Windows including working with file attributes from the Windows environment (and also from a command prompt) and managing environment variables as used in the internal properties of Windows links/shortcuts.  Appendix H details the process using one of the provided file projects as an example.\par
\par
An additional feature of the PEPS package is to readily work with any one file in the generic \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  The only limitation is that access is limited to the currently designated file as the commands are generic to all such files.  This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the Current_Program_Files directory such as \ul Set Current\i  \i0 Program\ulnone  and \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone .\par
\par
\par
Any user of the PEPS package can easily work with any number of files, changing focus between active files as necessary.  If the generic commands method is used, the only restriction is the use of a single file at a time and limiting file storage to a specific directory.  This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory such as \ul Set Current\i  \i0 Program\ulnone  and \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone .\par
\par
  The advantage for this section is setting up a particular file for development can be accomplished quickly and easily; the particular file method requires considerable effort to create the command shortcuts. \par
\par
\par
\par
Any user of the PEPS package can easily work with any number of files, changing focus between active files as necessary.  If the generic commands method is used, the only restriction is the use of a single file at a time and limiting file storage to a specific directory.  This process is documented in section 1.50 below regarding commands related to the \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory such as \ul Set Current\i  \i0 Program\ulnone  and \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone .\par
