        Figure 3.  Inactive Programs directory\par
\par
As shown in Figure 3 above, the \i I\ul nactive Programs\ulnone\i0  directory (accessible from the main Command directory) contains a pair of command shortcut/links that can be moved to the main Command directory as necessary; this can be accomplished using standard Windows Explorer techniques such as \ul\i Cut\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Copy\ulnone\i0  and \ul\i Paste\ulnone\i0 .  These example commands are completely operational; the associated command files (\ul\i Edit and Copy 8\ulnone\i0 the \ul\i SIMH Batch Files\ulnone\i0  directory as the PEPS package requires.\par
\par
Note: Due to specific quirks of Windows, embedded space characters may be placed within the shortcut text to achieve better visual results. This is often a trial and error process specific to the display method chosen; in this example medium-sized icons are used.  Other display options such as \i List\i0  or \i Details\i0  or\i  Tiles\i0  will tend to have different requirements.\par
\par
This example is particular to the project file named \i PROGRAM.PAL\i0 .  Other projects can be defined using alrernate file names.  There is no practical limit to the number of independent project files; however, the creation of shortcut/links and underlying support files is an advanced process to be accomplished by advanced users of the PEPS package.  Appendix H details the underlying batch files that implement the PROGRAM.PAL file project including the arcane details of Windows that must be followed for a successful project.\par
\par
Note: Particular releases of the PEPS package may include additional command/shortcut links and associated support files; these files are provided by other PEPS package users to assist in creating new custom project files.\par
\par
The use of the PROGRAM.PAL project is described as follows:\par
\par
1) Edit PROGRAM.PAL.  This command brings up the source file\par
   \i\\PDP-8\\Current_Program_Files\\PROGRAM.PAL\i0  for editing in the\par
   Windows \i Notepad\i0  program.  Editing changes can be made at any time.\par
   Either saving the file within the editor or exiting the editor is\par
   necessary to proceed in the development process of this file.\par
\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1    Note: Appendix C contains information regarding conforming\par
   alternate editing programs which can be substituted for Windows\par
   Notepad on an individualized basis, as well as potential feature\par
   tradeoffs that may result.  These considerations apply to all file\par
   editing situations throughout the PEPS environment.  It is strongly\line    recommended only advanced users modify the editing program\par
   parameters to call up any particular file editing alternative due\par
   to potential interaction with open files and other running tasks\par
   (such as the SIMH simulator).  In certain instances, the host\par
   Windows release (32-bit or 64-bit) may also be a factor.\par
\pard\par
   If the editor is closed, the contents of the PROGRAM.PAL file is\par
   copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file which, in turn, is used as the\par
   dedicated input to the simulated paper-tape reader device (PTR:)\par
   within the SIMH simulator environment.  Once this is accomplished,\par
   any of the supported operating systems can be brought up; utilities\par
   within each operating system can then process the simulated paper-\par
   tape input followed by any desired additional utilities (such as\par
   calling up an assembler program).\par
\par
2) Send PROGRAM.PAL.  This command copies the contents of the\par
   PROGRAM.PAL file to the PTRIN.TXT file as described in 1) above,\par
   then a designated operating system is launched in a SIMH session.\par
   Any supported operating system can be specified for this operation.\par
   However, the same operating system is always used when executing\par
   this command.\page    Because the PROGRAM.PAL file is always copied to the PTRIN.TXT file\par
   when using the \ul Send PROGRAM.PAL\ulnone  command, it is not necessary to\par
   close the editing session, which can stay active in a separate\par
   window.  By using this method, it is possible to achieve rapid\par
   program file development; the editing session need only be closed\par
   when done with all development operations.\par
\par
   Note: To use multiple operating systems to process the same file,\par
   it is necessary to close the editing session as described above in\par
   1).  At that point, opening a new SIMH session to any operating\par
   system (which does not specify any editing or other modification of\par
   the PTRIN.TXT file) will pass the last edited file contents in the\par
   PTRIN.TXT file.  This can be accomplished repeatedly since, in\par
   general, only closing editing sessions or send command execution\par
   will change the PTRIN.TXT file.  This can be useful when comparing\par
   operating system environments such as OS/8 and P?S/8 when passed\par
   the same file or taking advantage of features unique to each\par
   operating system.\par
\par
The underlying implementation of these two commands are two related batch files called \ul\i Edit and Copy PROGRAM.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  and \ul\i Send PROGRAM.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  stored in the \ul\i SIMH Batch Files\ulnone\i0  directory.  These files (and the shortcuts/links to them generally placed in the Command directory when actively used) need to be adapted to create an equivalent set of batch files and shortcuts/links for each user-defined file; the subject file illustrated here is only provided as a working example.  Note: Additional files may be provided in the \ul Inactive Programs\ulnone  directory based on the works of other users.\par
\par
The user can remove all such unused components as necessary to avoid clutter when adding in new user-defined components.  Note: It is recommended that unneeded shortcuts/links be moved to the \ul Inactive Programs\ulnone  directory already provided for this purpose; the underlying batch files require very little storage and might be useful for future user file development projects.\par
\par
Note: The PROGRAM.PAL file, as provided in the PEPS package, is a dummy file intended for use by those who want to quickly develop a short program in \ul\i PDP-8 Assembly Language\ulnone\i0  (PAL).  By foregoing a distinctive name, any user can quickly develop this file as far as deemed necessary.  As required, the file can later be copied to another directory (and renamed as the user wishes) using Windows Explorer techniques.  \par
\par
Depending on requirements, future work can be initiated in a variety of ways.  For slightly larger projects, it is recommended that the user deploy the \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory technique as referenced above and described in further detail in Section 1.62 below.  While this requires some slight additional setup effort on the part of the user, this is easily mastered by beginners after some minimal experience with the PEPS environment.\par
\par
Advanced users can create custom command pairs patterned after the method used to develop PROGRAM.PAL while applying their own unique filenames.  Using additional advanced techniques, files can be located somewhere within the recommended file structure group (\i PDP-8\\Source Files\i0 ) or on other Windows directories/devices as deemed appropriate.\page Appendix H documents the relevant parameters that must be set to implement any of these methods; not every project should necessarily be setup using the same overall method.  Short-term projects using PROGRAM.PAL can be used instantly while named files residing in the designated current program files directory may describe a multiple file project. Only the most advanced projects may require resorting to the custom-named files method.\par
\par
Each method has a slightly different set of tradeoffs; as user experience grows, it will become apparent which approach serves best for any programming project.  In certain cases, working with source files created by others would contemplate little to no file editing once the proper file contents are confirmed.  The custom command pairs method would generally be too tedious in this situation; copying all such files to the current program files directory would likely suffice.\par
\par
\ul 1.50\tab Operating the PEPS from the Command directory\ulnone\par
\par
The Command directory arrangement (shown in Figure 3 below) includes all commands associated with the current release.  This includes working commands provided as examples illustrating host editing of specified text files and the transmission of updated files into the SIMH environment running one of the supplied operating systems.  Additional commands are provided to process various files returned from the SIMH environment as well as perform other functions as described below.\par
\par
The specific details of the Command window are subject to user preference considerations with regard to approaching the Windows desktop.  The commands (as shown in Figure 3 below) are configured according to certain Windows parameters and other factors currently in effect:\par
 \par
\tab a) Medium icons are in use; other available alternatives include \tab    both smaller and larger icons, full details and a larger tiled\par
\tab    format; some of these alternatives will fully display the\par
\tab    captions labeling all icons regardless of label character\par
\tab    count (albeit likely occupying a larger portion of the overall\par
\tab    Windows desktop).\par
\par
\tab    Note: In this example, one of the icons was deliberately\par
\tab    selected (thus the displayed color is darker) for the purpose\par
\tab    of expanding the longer caption text.  Due to the way Windows\par
\tab    operates, when not selected, the caption text may become\par
\tab    truncated unless one of the other view alternatives is used.\par
\par
  \tab    In general, most of the captions are completely shown even\par
  \tab    when not selected; the medium icons and supplied captions\par
 \tab    represent a reasonable compromise of readability and clutter\par
 \tab    avoidance.\page\tab b) All command icons are positioned by asserting \ul\i Align to Grid\ulnone\par
\i0\tab    with the associated \ul\i Auto Arrange\ulnone\i0  option disabled.  Settings to\par
\tab    accomplish this are available within the window view\par
\tab    particulars available in all supported Windows versions\par
\tab    (perhaps requiring a small patch to the desktop controls).\par
\par
\tab    Note: Third-party patches for the affected systems are\par
\tab    described in Appendix I.\par
\par
\tab c) The grouping of icons in the Command window (as illustrated in \tab    Figure 3 below) is primarily by similarity of function.  As\par
\tab    necessary, empty positions are deliberately reserved for the\par
\tab    purpose of future expansion.  Additional commands will likely\par
\tab    be added in future releases.  Several examples of specific\par
\tab    file editing and transmitting the updated file to the SIMH\par
\tab    simulator are provided.  Additionally, a series of commands is\par
\tab    provided to implement generic file processing requiring no\par
\tab    changes to the Command window.\par
\par
\tab d) The general icon color scheme chosen includes specific colors\par
\tab    for related functions as well as colors associated with two of\par
\tab    the supported operating systems (OS/8 and P?S/8 configured\par
\tab    without limitations).  These color cues assist the user in\par
\tab    selecting the desired function.  Advanced users can modify any\par
\tab    of these considerations; however, beginners are advised to\par
\tab    work with the standard release as documented here.\par
\par
Note: Icon organization within the Command window must be accomplished using Windows commands to allow icon position to be determined manually; icon alignment is recommended for an orderly appearance.  For Windows releases past Windows Vista, slight system modifications may be necessary.  Appendix J contains information on utilities that may be required.\line\line Undoubtedly, future releases of the PEPS package will contain numerous additional icons for a variety of reasons (such as variations of system configurations for the various supported operating systems).  As the package evolves, various methods will be suggested for removing clutter in several different categories.  Perhaps families of similar operating systems differing only in boot device specifics or handler configuration can be stored in an auxiliary window stored within the Command window.  The user can place only the most frequently used configurations into the easiest accessible window positions.\par
\par
Note: For the convenience of the user, the PEPS package includes an icon/shortcut to the Command directory, which is intended to be placed on the main desktop.  By opening the Command directory, most PEPS features are immediately available (including easy access to specific auxiliary directories as needed).\par
