 Figure 3.  PEPS Command Directory Window\par
\par
Advanced users are encouraged to create custom icons for new command shortcuts/links to be added to the Command directory; new icon files must be placed in the existing icon directory (\i Links and Support Files)\i0  prior to editing shortcut properties (generally using a copy of an existing shortcut/link as a template).  This will greatly simplify the editing process; otherwise, Windows acts in a presumptive manner which can be quite frustrating.  Note: This is a severe limitation of Windows that cannot (easily) be overcome.\page By using the provided directory for all icon files, the path strings within the shortcut can be safely edited changing only the trailing characters in the path string (which is the icon filename) while maintaining the original leading portion of the path string.  More information on creating custom shortcuts/links is available in Appendix H.\par
\par
\ul 1.60\tab Description of individual commands within the Command Window\ulnone\par
\par
The six rows of the command window shown in Figure 3 above are described below, each in left to right order.  With few exceptions, each command is a shortcut/link to a corresponding batch command file stored in the \ul SIMH Batch Files\ulnone  directory.\par
\par
\ul 1.61\tab Supported Operating Systems\ulnone\par
\par
As shown in Figure 3 above, commands to launch various operating systems are located in the top row of the Command directory as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) OS/8.  This is generally OS/8 Version 3D (also known as OS/8\par
\tab    Version 3Q).  In specific instances, system program components\par
\tab    have been patched to correct known issues.  Certain additional\par
\tab    programs from newer OS/8 family releases are included that are\par
\tab    generally improvements on the original releases.  While there\par
\tab    may be minor quirks or other performance trade-offs using\par
\tab    these programs, this should be of no practical consequence\par
\tab    when used in the PEPS environment.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Some of the added programs merely fail to be usable on\par
\tab    real-world hardware systems prior to the PDP-8/E; since the\par
\tab    SIMH simulator supports all instructions from the PDP-8/E\par
\tab    (KK8F) CPU, this is of no consequence.  Users intending to\par
\tab    transfer the system images contained in the PEPS package to\par
\tab    real-world hardware are advised of the inherent limitations.\par
\par
  \tab    Certain non-DEC programs are also provided to perform\par
\tab    additional tasks, such as the Kermit-12 utilities written by\par
\tab    Charles Lasner.  These programs are needed to process specific\par
\tab    files obtained externally from other users usually by means of\par
\tab    the Internet.\par
\par
\tab    The included configuration of OS/8 boots to the RK8E/RK05,\par
\tab    generally mounted on RK05 unit 0.  By changing the\par
\tab    configuration of mounted drives, it is possible to boot OS/8\par
\tab    on any RK05 drive 0-3 using supported techniques of the SIMH\par
 \tab    simulator or running short programs from another operating\par
\tab    system.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the host Windows environment passes \tab    files to the SIMH simulator, it is necessary to include the\par
\tab    standard OS/8 PTR: handler to process paper-tape input unless\par
\tab    P?S/8 is booted first.\page\tab    When finished, P?S/8 will include the \i PTUTIL\i0  program that does\par
\tab    not have the program defect described below.  (Note: PTUTIL is\par
\tab    the latest P?S/8 utility currently under development at the\par
\tab    time of this writing.)  After the PTR: input is correctly\par
\tab    processed by P?S/8, the P?S/8 \i OS8CON \i0 utility can be used to\par
\tab    pass the resultant file to an OS/8 device (such as DTA1:)\par
\tab    available in the OS/8 configuration.\par
\par
\tab    Because of a known quirk of the OS/8 PTR: handler, the very\par
\tab    last character of the simulated PTR: character stream\par
 \tab    (contents of the \i PTRIN\i0  file in the \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0\par
\tab    directory) will be ignored; as such, any file obtained from\par
\tab    the PTR: handler will be slightly truncated.  If the passed\par
 \tab    file is an ASCII text file, the lost input will generally be a\par
 \tab    trailing <LF> character (which can be restored by passing the\par
  \tab    file through OS/8 \i PIP\i0  or various editing programs such as OS/8\par
 \tab    \i TECO\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab    Certain programs which process <CR> characters and ignore <LF>\par
\tab    characters will function correctly when reading these files\par
\tab    despite the slight defect.  Binary files (which generally have \tab    multiple leader/trailer characters) will generally not be\par
\tab    affected by the PTR: handler quirk.\par
\par
\tab    Additional bootable OS/8 systems can be added that can access \tab    other devices besides the RK8E/RK05 to support such as the\par
 \tab    RX8E/RX01 or boot to other devices such as the TD8E DECtape\par
\tab    (or support alternate non-system handlers); it is the nature\par
 \tab    of OS/8 that many viable system configurations can be\par
\tab    supported.  Additional configuration files and program\par
\tab    shortcut/links can be added to the PEPS package as desired.\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8.  This is Version 8Z of P?S/8 configured for the\par
 \tab    TC01/TC08 DECtape with up to eight logical drives numbered 0\par
\tab    through 7.  Over the years, various patches have been applied\par
\tab    to P?S/8 Version 8Z to correct certain known bugs.  An\par
\tab    \ul\i Engineering Change Order\ulnone\i0  (\i ECO\i0 ) log file is available to detail\par
\tab    what has been modified since the original release of Version\par
\tab    8Z on 23-Feb-1988.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Future support of P?S/8 within the PEPS environment will\par
\tab    be upgraded with the release of P?S/8 Version 9A which is\par
\tab    currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    The PTUTIL and \i BINCON\i0  utilities will be added to Version 8Z\par
\tab    when available, likely using the temporary system program\par
\tab    execution method described within various P?S/8 user guides.\par
 \tab    These will be included in the future P?S/8 Version 9A release\par
\tab    as normal system programs.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Both OS/8 and P?S/8 are capable of booting any available\par
 \tab    operating systems within PEPS directly using simple commands\par
\tab    or by running short boot-up programs which are provided in\par
\tab    each system or are documented within Appendix J and can be\par
\tab    trivially created manually.\page\tab    In the case of the RK8E/RK05 cartridge disk device, this will\par
\tab    allow boot-up to any of the four possible drives if a suitable\par
\tab    cartridge file is mounted by proper inclusion in the relevant\par
\tab    configuration file(s).  All intersystem bootup procedures are\par
\tab    documented in Appendix J.\par
\par
\tab c) P?S/8 with the lineprinter disabled.  In earlier releases of\par
        P?S/8, many programs automatically directed output to the\par
        device 66 lineprinter if it was available and online.  To\par
        prevent this action, it was necessary to disable the\par
 \tab    lineprinter either by powering down or placing the device in\par
\tab    an offline state.\par
\par
\tab    As of the release of P?S/8 Version 8Z, most of these problems\par
        have been eliminated; command-line option switches exist in \par
\tab    the affected programs that force output to the console\par
\tab    terminal if desired.  To allow testing of any remaining\par
\tab    problems, P?S/8 may be started with the device 66 lineprinter\par
\tab    logically disabled in the corresponding SIMH configuration\par
\tab    file.\par
\par
\tab    While fully supported, this system variation is of little\par
\tab    interest to most PEPS users; once all known issues are\par
  \tab    resolved, this mode of operation will likely be withdrawn from\par
\tab    future PEPS releases or moved to the Inactive Commands\par
\tab    sub-directory.\par
\par
\tab Note: Future releases of the PEPS package will include additional \tab operating systems such as P?S/8 configured for the TD8E/TU56 \tab (possibly requiring the MR8E-C ROM), RK8E/RK05 or RX8E/RX01.\par
\par
\tab As the Command directory window becomes more complex, it may \tab become necessary to include an internal folder for choice of \tab operating system to avoid clutter.  Advanced users may also\par
\tab require internal folders containing additional file development\par
\tab icons/links (other than a few currently present in the main\par
\tab folder) to further limit clutter.  With experience, all users can\par
\tab determine which Windows desktop particulars serve their needs in\par
\tab an efficient manner; consider the provided Command directory (as\par
\tab shown above in figure 3) as merely a starting point which can\par
\tab evolve as requirements change.\par
\par
\ul 1.62\tab Generic file processing and related commands\ulnone\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown above in Figure 3, all generic file processing and \tab related commands are located in the second row of the Command \tab directory.\par
\par
\tab The PEPS environment includes an internal folder for files \tab currently under development which is referenced as the\par
\i\tab\i0 Current_Program_Files directory.  This is in sharp contrast with\par
\tab files stored in a more general collection of external directories \tab organized by user preference; a partial implementation of these \tab directories is provided with each release of the PEPS package\par
 \tab including certain example files that are accessible with explicit\par
 \tab commands.  This file structure should serve as a guide to\par
 \tab additional user directory organization and file development.\page\tab Commands described in this section are available to interact with\par
\tab files in both the Current_Program_Files directory and the\par
\tab external directories using Windows Explorer techniques; most of\par
\tab these commands are geared towards operating on files within the\par
\tab Current_Program_Files\i  \i0 directory.\par
\par
\tab a) Edit Current Program.  The current named file is opened in a\par
\tab    new window using the designated editor, in this case Windows\par
\tab    Notepad.  The Current Program filename is set using the \ul Set\ulnone\i\par
 \tab    \ul\i0 Current Program\ulnone  command as described in c) below.\par
 \par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    Note: Appendix C contains information regarding conforming\par
\pard\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves the updated state\par
\tab    of the current named file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file, the contents of which are used as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device within the SIMH simulator environment.  This\par
\tab    allows any operating system to be launched with the current\par
\tab    named file already set up to be read in when needed; however,\par
\tab    it is not necessary to close the editing program:\par
\par
\tab    If the current named file is saved using appropriate commands\par
\tab    within the editor, the \ul Send Current Program\ulnone  command described\par
\tab    in b) below can be used to process the file in the designated\par
\tab    operating system perhaps followed by other programs deemed\par
\tab    necessary for development in both the simulated and host\par
\tab    environments; as required, this process can be repeated.  This\par
\tab    is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 programs taking\par
\tab    advantage of the host environment file structure and certain\par
\tab    relevant PEPS features described elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\tab    Note: All aspects of the \ul Edit Current Program\ulnone  command are\par
\tab    defined in the \ul\i Edit Current Program.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in the \ul\i S\i0 IMH Batch\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \ul\i0 Files\ulnone\i  \i0 directory.\par
 \par
\tab b) Send Current Program.  The contents of the current named file\par
        are copied to the PTRIN.TXT file used as the input to the PTR:\par
        device within the SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is\par
 \tab    then launched in a separate window, booting to the designated\par
 \tab    operating system.  The Current Program filename is set using\par
\tab    the \ul Set Current Program\ulnone  command described in c) below.\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\par
\pard\tab    Note: All aspects of the \ul Send Current Program\ulnone  command are\par
\tab    defined in the \ul\i Send Current Program.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in the \ul SIMH Batch\i\par
\ulnone  \tab    \ul\i0 Files\ulnone\i  \i0 directory.\par
\par
  \tab    As released, P?S/8 with standard LPT: support is the\par
\tab    designated operating system for use with this command.  By\par
\tab    making a small change to the \ul Send Current Program.BAT\ulnone  file,\par
\tab    any available operating system can be chosen.  Several\par
\tab    operating system choices are already provided within the\par
\tab    batch file; all inactive choices should be preceded with a \i REM\i0\par
\tab    statement to simplify further modifications in the future.\page\tab c) Set Current Program.  The Current Program filename (as\par
\tab    referenced in a) and b) above) is set using this command.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The Current Program filename text string is encoded and\par
\tab    permanently stored in the installed PEPS package within a key\par
\tab    parameter file.  This command is the only method available to\par
\tab    update the filename; this applies even if the entire PEPS\par
\tab    package is repackaged for transport to another Windows system.\par
\par
\tab    As shown in Figure 4 below, the default Current Program file\par
 \tab    name is presently \i HELLOWORLD.PAL\i0 .  This command allows the\par
\tab    filename to be changed to any desired combination of\par
\tab    alphanumeric characters in upper or lower case; while embedded\par
\tab    space characters are not allowed, several additional\par
\tab    characters (such as \cf1\highlight2\i [\cf0\highlight0\i0  and \cf1\highlight2\i ]\cf0\highlight0\i0 ) are permitted.\par
   \f2\fs22\par
