\par
\tab f) Explore Additional Program Files.  A separate window is opened\par
\tab    to the \i PDP-8\\Source Files\i0  directory.  It is recommended that\par
\tab    all files oriented towards PDP-8 projects be organized into\par
\tab    appropriate sub-folders of this directory.  Files stored in\par
\tab    any of these directories can be interchanged with those of\par
 \tab    the Current_Program_Files directory using Windows Explorer\par
 \tab    techniques.\par
\par
\ul 1.63\tab Custom commands for individual file development\ulnone\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3 above, all custom commands for \tab individual file development are located in the third row of the\par
\tab Command directory window.\par
\par
\tab Some of the command examples supplied in this section are \tab associated with the OS/8 operating system while others are\par
     associated with the P?S/8 operating system.  While this generally\par
 \tab pertains to which operating system is chosen when sending the\par
     file via the PTRIN.TXT mechanism, the corresponding command\par
\tab icons/links are color-coded for consistency.  Black icons are\par
\tab selected for files to be developed using OS/8 while blue icons\par
\tab are selected for files to be developed using P?S/8.\par
\par
\tab Advanced users can derive commands for development of additional\par
\tab files using any desired color scheme (or alternate icon set). \tab Appendix E provides information for advanced users to aid in\par
\tab creating new batch files and corresponding command shortcut/icon\par
\tab entries.  The provided files described in this section can be\par
\tab used as templates to create custom additions to the standard\par
\tab installation.\page\tab Note: \i COROUTINE.PAL\i0  is a example of how to implement efficient\par
\tab coroutines taking good advantage of the PDP-8 instruction set.\par
\tab This is a code fragment taken from the main \i Kermit-12\i0  program\par
\tab written by Charles Lasner as part of the overall Kermit-12 \tab project.\par
\par
\tab a) Edit COROUTINE.PAL.  The text file \i\\\ul PDP-8\\Source Files\\Generic\ulnone\par
 \tab    \ul Source Files\\COROUTINE.PAL\ulnone\i0  is opened in a new window using the\par
\tab    designated editor, in this case Windows Notepad.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Appendix C contains information regarding conforming\par
\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\pard\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves the updated state\par
\tab    of the COROUTINE.PAL file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    PTRIN.TXT file, the contents of which are used as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device within the SIMH simulator environment.  This\par
\tab    allows any operating system to be launched with the\par
\tab    COROUTINE.PAL file already set up to be read in when needed.\par
\tab    However, it is not necessary to close the editing program:\par
\par
\tab    If the COROUTINE.PAL file is saved using appropriate commands\par
\tab    within the editor, the \ul Send COROUTINE.PAL\ulnone  command described in\par
\tab    b) below can be used to process the file in the designated\par
\tab    operating system perhaps followed by other programs deemed\par
\tab    necessary for development in both the simulated and host\par
\tab    environments; as required, this process can be repeated.  This\par
\tab    is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 programs taking \par
\tab    advantage of the host environment file structure and certain\par
\tab    relevant PEPS features described elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    Note: All particulars of the \ul Edit COROUTINE.PAL\ulnone  command are\par
 \tab    defined within the \ul\i Edit and Copy COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in the\par
\tab    \ul SIMH Batch Files\ulnone  directory.\par
\pard\par
\tab b) Send COROUTINE.PAL.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Generic Source Files\\COROUTINE.PAL\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    PTRIN.TXT file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system.\par
\par
  \tab    As released, OS/8 is the designated operating system for use\par
\tab    with this command.  By making a small change to the \ul\i Send \par
\ulnone         \ul COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file, any available operating system can be\par
\tab    chosen.  Several operating system choices are already provided\par
\tab    within the batch file; all inactive choices should be preceded\par
\tab    with a REM statement to simplify further modifications in the\par
\tab    future.\par
\par
\tab    Note: All aspects of the \ul Send COROUTINE.PAL\ulnone  command are\par
\tab    defined in the \ul\i Send COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in the \ul SIMH Batch\i\par
\ulnone  \tab    \ul\i0 Files\ulnone  directory.\par
\par
\tab    Since both a) and b) (as described above) refer to a\par
\tab    file developed within the OS/8 environment, the associated\par
\tab    icons for these commands are black color.\page\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab c) Edit MEMORYSIZE.PAL.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\tab    \\Generic\\Source Files\\MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  is opened in a new window\par
\tab    using the designated editor, in this case Windows Notepad.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Appendix C contains information regarding conforming\par
\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\pard\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves the updated state\par
\tab    of the MEMORYSIZE.PAL file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    PTRIN.TXT file, the contents of which are used as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device within the SIMH simulator environment.  This\par
\tab    allows any operating system to be launched with the\par
\tab    MEMORYSIZE.PAL file already set up to be read in when needed.\par
\tab    However, it is not necessary to close the editing program:\par
\par
\tab    If the MEMORYSIZE.PAL file is saved using appropriate commands\par
\tab    within the editor, the \ul Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL\ulnone  command described\par
\tab    in d) below can be used to process the file in the designated\par
\tab    operating system perhaps followed by other programs deemed\par
\tab    necessary for development in both the simulated and host\par
\tab    environments; as required, this process can be repeated.  This\par
\tab    is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 programs taking \par
\tab    advantage of the host environment file structure and certain\par
\tab    relevant PEPS features described elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    Note: All particulars of the \ul Edit MEMORYSIZE.PAL\ulnone  command are\par
 \tab    defined within the \ul\i Edit and Copy MEMORYSIZE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in\par
\tab    the \ul SIMH Batch Files\ulnone  directory.\par
\pard\par
\tab d) Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Generic Source Files\\MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    PTRIN.TXT file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system.\par
\par
  \tab    As released, OS/8 is the designated operating system for use\par
\tab    with this command.  By making a small change to the \ul\i Send\ulnone\i0  \par
\tab    \ul\i MEMORYSIZE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file, any available operating system can be\par
\tab    chosen.  Several operating system choices are already provided\par
\tab    within the batch file; all inactive choices should be preceded\par
\tab    with a REM statement to simplify further modifications in the\par
\tab    future.\par
\par
\tab    Note: All aspects of the \ul Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL\ulnone  command are\par
\tab    defined in the \ul\i Send COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0  file in the \ul SIMH Batch\i\par
\ulnone  \tab    \ul\i0 Files\ulnone\i  \i0 directory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since both c) and d) (as described above) refer to a\par
\tab    file developed within the OS/8 environment, the associated\par
\tab    icons for these commands are black color.\par
\par
\tab    An OS/8-specific SIMH session using the contents of\par
\tab    MEMORYSIZE.PAL passed as the input to the PTR: device is shown\par
\tab    in Figure 8 below.  The various steps taken are documented as \tab    follows:\page\tab    i)   The OS/8 date is set to 06-Nov-88.  This date was chosen\par
 \tab         because it allows the day of the week to correspond to\par
\tab         that which applies on 06-Nov-2016.  OS/8 dates are not\par
\tab\tab    preserved across SIMH bootup sessions.\par
\par
\tab\tab    Note: OS/8 only allows dates in the range of 01-Jan-1970\par
\tab\tab    through 31-Dec-2001 with eight-year group anomalies for\par
\tab\tab    all dates past 31-Dec-1977.\par
\par
\tab    ii)  The OS/8 \i PIP.SV\i0  utility is executed to create the file\par
\tab         \i MEMSIZ.PA\i0  from the paper-tape input stream setup when the\par
\tab\tab    SIMH session was initiated.\par
\par
\tab    iii) The OS/8 \i PAL8.SV\i0  utility is executed to assemble the\par
\tab\tab    contents of the \i MEMSIZ.PA\i0  file into the \i MEMSIZ.BN\i0  binary\par
\tab         file.\par
\par
\tab    iv)  The OS/8 \i ABSLDR.SV\i0  utility is executed to load the\par
\tab\tab    \i MEMSIZ.BN \i0 binary file into memory; by specifying \i /G=200\i0 ,\par
\tab\tab    the program is automatically started.\par
\par
\tab    v)   The console output produced by program execution is shown\par
\tab\tab    in Figure 8 below.  Since the SIMH simulator is\par
\tab\tab    configured to provide a 32K memory environment, there are\par
\tab\tab    eight memory fields available numbered 0 through 7; the\par
\tab\tab    highest memory field is correctly indicated as 7.\par
\par
