\page\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab e) Edit WHATAMI.PAL.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\tab    \\Generic\\Source Files\\WHATAMI.PAL\i0  is opened in a new window\par
\tab    using Windows Notepad (the designated text editing program).\par
\tab    All particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit\par
 \tab    and Copy WHATAMI.PAL.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files\i0\par
\tab    directory.  As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current\par
\tab    Program\i0  command, Appendix C contains information regarding\par
\tab    conforming alternative editing programs.\par
\pard\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
 \tab    the \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  file; the contents of the file are copied\par
\tab    to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as input to the PTR: device during\par
\tab    the next SIMH simulator session.  This allows any available\par
\tab    operating system to be launched with the contents of\par
\tab    \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  already setup within the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file for\par
\tab    capture within the selected PDP-8 operating system.\par
\par
  \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time file updates are made.  Merely by\par
\tab    saving the file using an appropriate editor command, the \i Send\par
\tab    WHATAMI.PAL\i0  command (as described in f) below) can be used\par
 \tab    to initiate a SIMH session that can produce appropriate\par
\tab    results leading to another file edit cycle, etc.  This is the\par
\tab    fastest method to develop most PDP-8 programs taking advantage\par
\tab    of features of the host Windows environment.\par
\par
\tab f) Send WHATAMI.PAL.  The contents of the \ul\i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\ulnone\tab    \ul\\Generic Source Files\\WHATAMI.PAL\ulnone\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system\par
\tab    (as defined in the corresponding underlying batch file\par
\tab    associated with this command [\ul\i Send WHATAMI.PAL.BAT\ulnone\i0 ]).  Note:\par
\tab    While P?S/8 is the default operating system for this function,\par
\tab    a minor edit to this batch file can be made to cause a bootup\par
\tab    to OS/8 or other available operating system; this option\par
\tab    exists in all analogous batch files to provide free choice of\par
\tab    operating system when sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since both e) and f) (as described above) refer to a\par
\tab    file developed within the P?S/8 environment, the associated\par
\tab    icons for these commands are blue color.\par
\par
\tab    A P?S/8-specific SIMH session using the contents of\par
 \tab    \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  passed as the input to the PTR: device is shown\par
\tab    in Figure 9 below.  The various steps taken are documented as \tab    follows:\par
\par
\tab    i)   The P?S/8 date was set to 06-Nov-2016 in a prior SIMH\par
\tab         session.  Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 retains the current date\par
\tab         permanently until changed.\par
\par
\tab         Note: Date handling in P?S/8 Version 8Z can be extended\par
\tab\tab    through 31-Dec-2099 by applying maintenance patches \tab  \tab\tab    roughly every eight years.  Future releases will extend\par
\tab\tab    date handling through 31-Dec-2411 without requiring any\par
\tab\tab    maintenance procedures.\page\tab    ii)  The \i WHATAMI.PAL\i0  file was processed into the eight \i Tiny\par
\tab         File System\i0  (TFS) files \i WHT[1]\i0 , \i WHT[2]\i0 , \i WHT[3]\i0 , \i WHT[4]\i0 ,\par
\tab         \i WHT[5]\i0 , \i WHT[6]\i0 , \i WHT[7]\i0  and \i WHT[8]\i0  from the paper-tape\par
\tab         input stream setup in a prior SIMH session.\par
\par
\tab         Note: P?S/8 also supports extended-length files.  If this\par
\tab         method had been chosen, the program could have been\par
             accessed by using a single file name such as \i WHTAMI\i0 .\par
\par
\tab\tab    The eight TFS files are passed to the P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  assembler\par
\tab\tab    program; command-line option switches initiate the\par
\tab\tab    assembly process followed by an automatic chain to the\par
\tab         P/S/8 system program \i BIN\i0  which performs binary file\par
 \tab\tab    loading when used in this manner.  The default binary\par
\tab         file\i  % \i0 is used as the output target of the assembly and\par
\tab         then as the binary input file passed to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 .  Since\par
\tab\tab    a starting address is passed using \i /G=200\i0 , the program \tab\tab\tab    starts automatically after loading.\par
\par
\tab         Note: The assembler statistics indicate there were no\par
\tab         assembly errors.  Had there been any errors, the\par
\tab         automatic chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  would have been aborted.\par
\par
\tab    iii) The WHATAMI.PAL program is designed to detect the\par
\tab         specific PDP-8 hardware model the program is running on.\par
\tab\tab    Since the SIMH simulator is configured to support the\par
\tab\tab    KK8F CPU, the program indicates it is running on a\par
\tab\tab    PDP-8/E or similar model.\par
