\pard  Figure 9.  P?S/8 SIMH session using WHATAMI.PAL\page\tab g) Edit FOCAL Program.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Current_Program_Files\\PROGRAM.FOC\i0  is opened in a new window\par
\tab    using Windows Notepad (the designated text editing program).\par
\tab    All particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit\par
 \tab    and Copy FOCAL Program.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files\i0\par
\tab    directory.  As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current\par
\tab    Program\i0  command, Appendix C contains information regarding\par
\tab    conforming alternative editing programs.\par
\pard\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
 \tab    the \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file; the contents of the file are copied\par
\tab    to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as input to the PTR: device during\par
\tab    the next SIMH simulator session.  This allows any available\par
\tab    operating system to be launched with the contents of\par
\tab    \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  already setup within the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file for\par
\tab    capture within the selected PDP-8 operating system.\par
\par
  \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time file updates are made.  Merely by\par
\tab    saving the file using an appropriate editor command, the \i Send\par
\tab    PROGRAM.FOC\i0  command (as described in h) below) can be used\par
 \tab    to initiate a SIMH session that can produce appropriate\par
 \tab    results leading to another file edit cycle, etc.  This is the\par
\tab    fastest method to develop most FOCAL (and other PDP-8\par
 \tab    programs) taking advantage of features of the host Windows\par
\tab    environment.\par
\par
\tab h) Send FOCAL Program.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Current_Program_Files\\PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system\par
\tab    (as defined in the corresponding underlying batch file\par
\tab    associated with this command [\i Send FOCAL Program.BAT\i0 ]).  Note:\par
\tab    While P?S/8 is the default operating system for this function,\par
\tab    a minor edit to this batch file can be made to cause a bootup\par
\tab    to OS/8 or other available operating system; this option\par
\tab    exists in all analogous batch files to provide free choice of\par
\tab    operating system when sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since both g) and h) (as described above) refer to a\par
\tab    file developed within the P?S/8 environment, the associated\par
\tab    icons for these commands are blue color; however, since these\par
\tab    commands are oriented towards FOCAL programs, a somewhat\par
\tab    lighter shade of blue was chosen.\par
\par
\tab    The default contents of the \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file is an accurate\par
\tab    copy of the historic DEC \i King of Sumeria\i0  game known as\par
\tab    \i HAMURABI\i0 .  This file is also included in the\i\\PDP-8\\Source\par
\tab    Files\\PQS8 FOCAL - Related Files\i0  directory as \i HAMURABI.FOC\i0 .\par
\par
\tab    A P?S/8-specific SIMH session using the contents of \par
\i\tab    PROGRAM.FOC\i0  passed as the input to the PTR: device is shown\par
\tab    in Figure 10 below.  The various steps taken are documented as \tab    follows:\page\tab    i)   As described above, the paper-tape input stream setup in\par
\tab         a prior SIMH session is the contents of \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  which\par
\tab         in turn is a copy of \i HAMURABI.FOC\i0 .  As is usually the\par
\tab         case with most short FOCAL programs, the entire program\par
\tab\tab    was converted into a single TFS file which was named\par
\tab\tab    \i HMRABI\i0 .\par
\par
\tab    ii)  The \i HMRABI\i0  file is then passed to P?S/8 \i FOCAL\i0  which is a\par
\tab         binary compatible superset of the original DEC FOCAL,\par
\tab         1969 release.  The contents of passed binary and text\par
\tab         files are used to implement custom binary patches to\par
\tab         FOCAL and pass FOCAL programs and data respectively.  In\par
\tab\tab    this instance, no binary patches are required.\par
\par
\tab         Various command-line switches (both present and\par
\tab         deliberately omitted), control the precise way FOCAL\par
\tab         operates.  In this instance, the Sine and Arctangent\par
\tab         option groups are not loaded to maximize the memory space\par
\tab         allocated to the program, variables and pushdown stack.\par
\par
\tab         The source file contents do not echo on the console\par
\tab\tab    device during the loading process; this operation can be\par
\tab\tab    enabled by changing the command-line options. Automatic\par
\tab\tab    program startup (after displaying the FOCAL command\par
\tab\tab    prompt character) is also initiated by the command-line\par
\tab\tab    options selected.\par
\par
\tab    iii) The running program presents various options to the local \tab\tab    town manager (\i HAMURABI\i0 ) requiring some strategy as how\par
 \tab\tab    best to proceed allocating the town's resources.\par
\tab\tab    Execution was stopped by the use of the Control-C \tab\tab\tab\tab    character to abort the FOCAL program.\par
\par
\tab    iv)  Users very familiar with this version of the \i HAMURABI\par
\i0\tab\tab    game, as documented in various DEC handbooks, may notice\par
\tab\tab    subtle differences in the program output as shown in\par
\tab\tab    Figure 10 below.  The original FOCAL, 1969 supplies a\par
\tab\tab    leading \i =\i0  character on all numerical output; the output\par
\tab\tab    format would include an extra leading space character\par
\tab\tab    which tends to make the numeric values slightly harder to\par
\tab\tab    read.\par
\par
\tab         It is the general consensus of the PDP-8 FOCAL user\par
\tab\tab    community at large these features are quirks which should\par
\tab\tab    be suppressed; as such, P?S/8 FOCAL removes the affected\par
\tab\tab    characters by default.  Command-line options are\par
\tab\tab    available to restore either or both historic features if\par
\tab\tab    desired.\page\pard\qc\f2\fs22{\pict\wmetafile8\picwgoal8624\pichgoal6524 
