\pard\tab  Figure 10. P?S/8 FOCAL SIMH session using PROGRAM.FOC\par
\par
\ul 1.64 Utility commands for paper-tape and lineprinter devices\ulnone\par
\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab Note: As shown above in Figure 3, all special-purpose utility\par
 \tab commands are located in the fourth row of the Command directory\par
 \tab window.\par
\par
\tab These commands are permanent components of the PEPS package\par
\tab targeting files used to communicate between the host Windows\par
\tab environment and the SIMH simulated environment; the files are\par
\tab located in the \i\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory.  The \tab commands are used to implement various operations on these\par
\tab files as needed regardless of which supported operating system \tab is being used at the time.\par
\par
\tab File descriptions are as follows:\par
\par
\tab 1) \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  - This file is used to capture the output directed\par
\tab    to the device 66 lineprinter during any SIMH session.  Various\par
\tab    commands are implemented to manage the contents of this file.\par
\par
\tab    By convention, the contents of the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  are presumed to \tab    be printable text generally formatted for use with real-word\par
\tab    lineprinters; various provided utilities can post-process this\par
\tab    file for additional purposes.\page\tab 2) \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  - This file is used to capture the output directed \tab    to the high-speed punch (PTP:) device.  While the file\par
\tab    extension used might imply printable text contents, the actual\par
\tab    file contents could be non-printable binary coding depending\par
\tab    on specific circumstances.  Actual text contents can be opened\par
\tab    in any designated editing program capable of processing ASCII\par
\tab    text characters.  Windows Notepad is the default program used\par
\tab    throughout the PEPS package for such editing/display purposes.\par
\tab    On a case by case basis, advanced users can modify the\par
\tab    underlying command files to use an alternative text editing\par
\tab    program if desired.\par
\par
 \tab 3) \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  - This file is used to transmit input data to the\par
\tab    high-speed reader (PTR:) device.  As described above in 2),\par
\tab    the file extension might imply printable text contents.\par
\tab    However, the actual file contents could be binary data such as\par
\tab    a standard PDP-8 binary file in \i BIN\super [7]\nosupersub\i0  format.  In general, the\par
\tab    user determines the specific contents of this file as well as \tab    the set of utilities within the designated current operating\par
\tab    system that will process the file contents.\par
\par
\tab The various commands described in this section manage nearly all\par
\tab situations related to these key files.  In certain rare \tab instances, it may become necessary to use one of the \tab miscellaneous commands (described in section 1.65 below) to\par
\tab accomplish certain additional tasks using Windows Explorer.  This\par
\tab would generally concern transfer of files obtained via the\par
\tab Internet or other external resources.  Appendix Q contains more\par
\tab information on optional commands to be included in this section\par
\tab or currently under development.  If specific utilities are\par
\tab proposed and implemented, command links will be added to this\par
\tab section in future releases.\line\line\tab The commands available are as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Clear LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file is emptied.  This\par
\tab    command is needed to ensure there are no artifact characters\par
\tab    in the file resulting from activity in previous SIMH sessions.\par
\tab    If the purpose of the next SIMH session is to produce a\par
\tab    listing file directed to the device 66 LPT: device, this\par
\tab    command must be used before initiating the SIMH session.\par
\par
\tab b) Strip LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file can contain artifacts\par
\tab    created by imperfect operation of various output utilities in\par
\tab    the supported operating systems.  Left as created by the\par
\tab    issuing programs, the file may be compromised in the Windows\par
\tab    environment, especially if post-processing will be applied.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Some of the problems with undesired \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file\par
\tab    contents arise from necessary implementation restrictions\par
\tab    within the original real-world PDP-8 environment; certain\par
\tab    utilities will time out the LPT: device and then fall back on\par
\tab    the console printer should the LPT: not be available.  In some\par
\tab    instances, test characters used would have been ignored in the \tab    sense that no artifacts would appear on the printed page of a\par
\tab    real-world system lineprinter; however, the SIMH simulator\par
\tab    captures all characters sent to the LPT: device.\page\tab    As such, it is generally necessary to remove these unwanted\par
\tab    characters from the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file.  In certain situations,\par
\tab    the artifact characters, while present, are truly unfathomable\par
\tab    or only work with specific printer models yet were never\par
\tab    discovered during the economic lifetime of these operating\par
\tab    systems.  In the case of certain P?S/8 lineprinter quirks,\par
\tab    the original development environment often lacked access to a\par
\tab    lineprinter and the program merely implemented the best\par
\tab    guesswork of the programmer.  Some of these issues result in\par
\tab    wasting a sheet of lineprinter paper; the intended output\par
\tab    starts on the next page at the top of form point.\par
\par
\tab    To solve all of the problems discussed above (and arcane\par
\tab    additional issues not documented here), the PEPS package\par
\tab    includes the \i STRIP.TEC\i0  macro designed to work with \i TECOC\i0 , a\par
\tab    freeware implementation of DEC standard \i TECO\i0  for Windows.\par
\tab    Once a file is processed by \i STRIP.TEC\i0 , all such undesired\par
\tab    output is eliminated; the resultant file is now ready for\par
\tab    further processing such as input to a word-processor for the\par
\tab    purpose of printing the file in Windows.  In some cases, post-\par
\tab    processing might include conversion to PDF format for\par
\tab    distribution to a wider audience or file archiving.\par
\par
\tab    Running the \ul\i STRIP.TEC\ulnone\i0  macro on typical text files is a time-\tab\par
\tab    consuming process; the actual execution time depends on the\par
\tab    Windows system processing power, \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file length and\par
\tab    other considerations.  As shown below in Figure 11, a separate\par
\tab    window is created to display the stripping process; the\par
\tab    starting and ending times are displayed during the program\par
\tab    execution to aid the user in determining future processing\par
\tab    time should the file (or a subsequent version after a source\par
\tab    file edit) require additional stripping.\par
\par
\tab    While the example shown below in Figure 11 took just under 65\par
\tab    seconds to complete, it is not uncommon for very long assembly\par
\tab    listings to take over five minutes for conversion into a\par
\tab    properly formatted file when executed on many Windows systems.\par
