\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs24   Figure 11. LPTOUT.TXT file stripping session\page\tab c) Open LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate display of the\par
\tab    consequences of processing such as assembling a source file\par
\tab    currently under development.\par
\tab\par
\tab    Note: While there is generally no need to modify the\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file, editing operations are freely allowed; the\par
\tab    file may be printed if desired.  In many circumstances, the\par
\tab    font size must be reduced to obtain a complete display on the\par
\tab    Windows desktop without resorting to horizontal scrolling.\par
\tab    This can occur because language processing utilities add\par
\tab    additional content at the left margin of each source line.\par
\par
\tab    While alternative text editing programs may be useful for\par
\tab    typical editing purposes, most other qualifying editing\par
\tab    utilities may either cause all open text files to share a\par
\tab    common window (by selecting one of a set of multiple tabs) or\par
\tab    not support multiple font sizes in a single session.  Unless\par
\tab    the alternative program can support these additional features,\par
\tab    it is recommended that Windows Notepad be used.\par
\par
\tab d) Clear PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is emptied.  This\par
\tab    command is needed to ensure there are no artifact characters\par
\tab    in the file resulting from activity in previous SIMH sessions.\par
\tab    If the purpose of the next SIMH session is to send data to the\par
\tab    PTP: device, this command must be used before initiating the\par
\tab    SIMH session.\par
\par
\tab e) Strip PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is stripped of unwanted\par
\tab    artifacts; other than the change of target file from\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  to \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0 , the entire process as described\par
 \tab    above in b) applies to this command.\par
\par
\tab    It has been occasionally observed that the \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file\par
\tab    will contain a trailing form-feed character after the file has\par
\tab    been stripped of all other artifacts.  The root cause of this\par
\tab    minor issue has been determined to be an obscure quirk of the\par
\tab    OS/8 PTP: handler due to an intrinsic design problem within\par
\tab    the handler.  Notably, if the identical file is processed by\par
\tab    the OS/8 LPT: handler (into the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file) the problem\par
\tab    doesn't occur.  Should this situation be problematic, the\par
\tab    character removal can be accomplished manually using the \i Open\par
\tab    PTP Output\i0  command (as described below in f); alternatively,\par
\tab    the entire stripping process can be repeated to eliminate the\par
\tab    unwanted artifact.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the OS/8 PTP: handler functions,\par
\tab    while the form-feed character is the last such character in\par
\tab    the original file, it is not actually the sentinel character\par
\tab    of the file, generally surrounded by sequences of unwanted\par
\tab    characters requiring removal at a different stage of the\par
\tab    stripping process.  Future releases of the \i STRIP.TEC\i0  macro may\par
\tab    be able to remove this defect in more instances than as at\par
\tab    present.  (As of the current release of the stripping\par
\tab    utility, this artifact is already being removed in many cases,\par
\tab    albeit not in every case.)\page\tab f) Open PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    standard Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate\par
\tab    display of the consequences of processing such as assembling\par
\tab    the source file currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    Other than the change of target file from \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  to\par
\tab    \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0 , the entire process as described above in c)\par
 \tab    applies to this command.\par
\par
\tab g) Swap PTR and PTP Files.  The contents of the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  (PTR:)\par
\tab    and \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  (PTP:) files are interchanged.  This provides\par
 \tab    file interchange useful in specific circumstances.\par
\par
\tab    For example, binary files created in P?S/8 can be made\par
\tab    available for OS/8 usage (or vice-versa) using this command.\par
\tab    This allows OS/8 users to take advantage of the unique\par
\tab    features of P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  which supports additional functionality\par
\tab    beyond OS/8 PAL8 while maintaining total PAL language\par
\tab    compatibility.\par
\par
\tab    With the availability of the P?S/8 \i PTUTIL\i0  program, text files\par
\tab    may be directly interchanged between these operating systems  \tab    without involving the host Windows environment.  Note: Other\par
 \tab    methods are available for file interchange between these two\par
\tab    operating systems.  Appendix V describes a variety of methods,\par
\tab    some of which are currently under development.\par
\par
\tab h) Swap PTP and LPT Files.  The contents of the \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  (PTP:) \tab    and \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  (LPT:) files are interchanged.  This provides\par
 \tab    file interchange useful in specific circumstances.\line\line\tab    For example, data created on the paper-tape punch may be\par
\tab    directed to the device 66 LPT: output file for further\par
\tab    processing such as creation of a document in PDF format. \par
\pard\par
\ul 1.65 Miscellaneous commands\ulnone\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown above in Figure 3, all miscellaneous commands are\par
\tab located in the fifth row of the Command directory window.\par
\par
\tab a) SIMH Working Files.  The \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory is\par
\tab    opened in a separate window.  Using Windows Explorer\par
\tab    techniques, various files may be accessed for such purposes as\par
\tab    file interchange with external sources.\par
\par
\tab    This folder is the working directory for device image files.\par
\tab    By copying device image files here (and editing the SIMH\par
\tab    configuration files) the contents of device image files can be\par
\tab    made active in the SIMH environment.  Appendix Y details the\par
\tab    important files located in this directory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Normal activity of the various components of the PEPS\par
\tab    package will tend to produce temporary files in this\par
\tab    directory; in extreme cases, it may be necessary to manually \par
\tab    remove temporary files.  However, the majority of temporary\par
 \tab    filenames are internally recycled, largely avoiding any\par
        maintenance requirements.\page\tab b) SIMH Config Files.  The \i SIMH Configuration Files\i0  directory is\par
\tab    opened in a separate window.  Within this directory are the\par
\tab    configuration files used with each variant operating system\par
\tab    used with the SIMH simulator.  Changes made to these files can\par
\tab    greatly affect the way the corresponding operating systems\par
 \tab    function.\par
\par
\tab c) Inactive Programs.  The \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory is opened\par
\tab    in a separate window.  Generally, icon/link files are stored\par
\tab    here for programming projects not currently under development.\par
\tab    Several of these files are provided by advanced users of the\par
\tab    PEPS package for the purpose of developing their own PDP-8\par
\tab    programs.  All of the files are useful as templates for\par
\tab    creating additional personal program development commands.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The provided files in the \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory\par
\tab    will not be actionable without the underlying support files.\par
\tab    In some cases, the referenced source files are proprietary and\par
\tab    are not available with the standard release of the PEPS\par
\tab    package.  However, the mechanism to exercise the icon/link\par
\tab    files should generally be functional due to dummy files\par
\tab    provided in lieu of the original source files.\par
\par
\tab d) Convert LPT to PDF.  The contents of the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  (LPT:)\par
 \tab    file is converted to the file \i LPTOUT.PDF\i0  in the\par
\tab    \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory using the \i text2pdf\i0  freeware\par
\tab    program from VeryPDF.\line\line\tab    The output is presented in 11" x 17" wide lineprinter paper\par
\tab    format with a white background.  Any conforming Windows font\par
\tab    may be used; the default distribution font is Consolas 10.\par
\tab    Font particulars can be changed by modification of the\par
\tab    \i TXT2PDF.BAT\i0  program located in the \i SIMH_Utilities\i0  directory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Not all Windows-compatible fonts are fully compatible\par
\tab    with the VeryPDF program; advanced users may consider\par
\tab    carefully attempting to use alternate fonts.\par
\par
\tab    The key VeryPDF utility program file is not included in the\par
\tab    standard release of the PEPS package for legal reasons.  PEPS\par
\tab    users may obtain the file from the author(s) of VeryPDF.\par
\tab    Appendix Z provides further information on obtaining this file\par
\tab    and how to add it to a working copy of the PEPS package.\par
\par
\tab    Most PDF file readers can adjust the display size for easy\par
\tab    viewing and output to printers of varying sizes.  It is\par
\tab    strongly recommended that \ul\i Adobe Reader\ulnone  \i0 version\i  11.x\i0  be\par
\tab    installed on the host Windows system.  Appendix F contains\par
\tab    more information regarding recommended Windows add-on\par
\tab    utilities and recommended settings/patches.\par
\par
\tab e) Convert LPT to PDF [Greenbar].  The contents of the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  \par
\tab    (LPT:) file is converted to the file \i LPTOUT.PDF\i0  in the\par
\tab    \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory using the \i text2pdf\i0  shareware\par
\tab    program from DaVince Tools.\page\tab    The output is presented in 11" x 8.5" landscape compressed\par
\tab    lineprinter paper format with a greenbar background.  Output\par
\tab    font is permanently set to Courier Bold 9.\par
\par
\tab    Key DaVince Tools utility program files are not included in\par
\tab    the standard release of the PEPS package for legal reasons.\par
\tab    PEPS users may obtain the files from the author(s) of DaVince\par
\tab    Tools.  Appendix Z provides further information on obtaining\par
\tab    these files and how to add them to a working copy of the PEPS\par
\tab    package.\par
\par
\tab    Most PDF file readers can adjust the display size and\par
\tab    orientation for easy viewing and output to printers of varying\par
\tab    sizes.  It is strongly recommended that \ul Adobe Reader\ulnone  Version\par
\tab    11.x be installed on the host Windows system.  Appendix F\par
\tab    contains more information regarding recommended Windows add-on\par
\tab    utilities and recommended settings/patches.\par
\par
\tab f) Help Documents.  The \i SIMH Documents\i0  directory is opened in a\par
\tab    separate window, allowing access to a variety of documentation\par
\tab    files on various PEPS-related subjects.  Since most of the\par
\tab    files are in .PDF format, a suitable program such as \i Adobe\par
\tab    Reader\i0  must be installed.  Appendix U provides additional\par
\tab    information on the available documents.\par
\par
\tab g) Open Log File.  The SIMH simulator logging file is opened in\par
\tab    the standard Windows Notepad editor.  It may be useful to\par
\tab    excise and edit a section of the logging file to document some \tab    issue with SIMH operation.\par
\par
\tab h) Setup Log File.  As shown in Figure 12 below, SIMH session\par
\tab    logging can be enabled or disabled using this command.  The\par
 \tab    session logging file can also be cleared if desired.\par
\par
