\par
\cf0\f3 The R (or RU\{n\}) command specifying the program name \highlight3 $\highlight0  (or \highlight3 %\highlight0 ) executes the default core image as setup by the latest GET execution or equivalent.  (Note: This will function permanently unless some other system usage or command changes the contents of the \highlight3 %\highlight0  and \highlight3 $\highlight0  files.  As necessary, the GET operation can be repeated to refresh the default program image.)\par
\par
PTUTIL has been designed to be (nearly) compatible with the technique described above to allow quick addition to existing systems; this execution mechanism alternative was introduced in P?S/8 Version 8Y and is supported on all newer releases.  The majority of conforming system programs can be supported using this tentative mechanism in lieu of inclusion within the System Programs Directory (unless overlay structures are required).\page\cf2 Appendix A (continued)\par
\cf0\par
Usage restrictions.\par
\par
When using PTUTIL to create ASCII text paper-tape output, the default leader/trailer length (surrounding the content obtained from all input files) is 100 (0144 octal) frames.  This value can be changed using the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter which is normally expressed in the current keyboard monitor radix (which is generally decimal).  However, the execution attribute to maintain the current radix is only available to programs located in the System Programs Directory.  (The default value is to force octal radix).  As such, any usage of the =xxxx parameter as described here must be expressed in octal.\par
\par
End of Appendix F\par
\f0\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\f3 Appendix H - Disabling Auto-arrange in newer Windows releases\par
\par
Note: Before attempting any of the changes described here, this entire section should be carefully read and completely understood.  It is necessary to have some familiarity with the Windows \ul\i Regedit\ulnone\i0  utility to perform these tasks; as required, seek assistance from a knowledgeable advanced Windows user.\par
\par
As shown in Figure 3 above, it may be desirable to customize the organization of icons within the Command window (and perhaps other windows set to display in any of the available icon views) to enhance readability and lend structure.  To accomplish this, clicking on the \ul\i View\ulnone\i0  command within each window displays separate options for \ul\i Auto Arrange\ulnone\i0  (which should be disabled) and \ul\i Align to Grid\ulnone\i0  (which should be enabled); each option is indicated as enabled by a leading check mark where applicable.\par
\par
Unfortunately, these options were removed from standard release starting with Windows 7; in essence, the effect of both of these options is only available simultaneously and the separate option check-off display area has been deleted.\par
\par
A user-written registry patch is available to overcome this limitation which fully restores the classic options as implemented in Windows XP and Windows Vista.  Registry patch files \i disableautoarrange.reg\i0  (and a companion file \i undoautoarrangedisable.reg\i0  to revert Windows to the standard release) are available in the \ul\i Auto-arrange Disable\ulnone\i0  directory within the \ul\i Links and Support Files\ulnone\i0  directory; the master release file manifest within Appendix Z should be queried to locate these files as required.\par
\par
There are several steps involved as follows:\line\line 1) The \i Regedit\i0  registry editor program must be invoked manually to\par
   determine which specific override options are present on any\par
   particular system.  The specifics of system configuration and\par
   release may vary; as such, it is necessary to check all potential\par
   registry sub-keys that may be present.  No Windows release has more\par
   than three relevant sub-keys; the method used to delete each\par
   existing target sub-key is identical in all cases where the sub-key\par
   exists.  All three potential registry keys must be checked to\par
   ensure all of the potential sub-keys are removed.\par
\par
   Note: For Windows systems with multiple logins, each individual\par
   login may include a separate copy of one or more of the affected\par
   sub-keys; as such, it may be necessary to repeat the entire process\par
   for each login where the modifications described here are desired.\par
   The only purpose served by these sub-keys is to impose the release\par
   defaults; they can always be safely removed.  An alternate registry\par
   patch is provided to restore the original registry settings if the\par
   changes described here are later deemed undesirable; when this\par
   reversion patch is applied, all of the sub-keys are recreated\par
   automatically at the next login when attempting to set any of the\par
   \ul\i View\ulnone\i0  options.\page\cf2 Appendix H (Continued).\par
\cf0\par
2) After all potential sub-keys described above are deleted, Windows\par
   must be rebooted.  At this point, none of the potential conditions\par
   that affect icon placement are in effect.  To ensure the options\par
   can be applied separately, it is necessary at this point to apply\par
   the \i disableautoarrange.reg\i0  registry patch file contents; this is\par
   generally accomplished by selecting the \i Merge\i0  option that appears\par
   when performing a right-click on the file name.\par
\par
3) Using \ul\i Windows Explorer\ulnone\i0  (known in some systems as \ul\i Windows File\par
\ulnone\i0    \ul\i Explorer\ulnone\i0 ), the main system directory (known as \ul\i Computer\ulnone\i0  in Windows \par
   7 and \ul\i This PC\ulnone\i0  in newer editions) should be opened and closed.\par
\par
4) After the system is rebooted, the options should be restored in all\par
   icon-based views.\par
\par
Removing the affected sub-keys.\par
\par
There are three potential registry sections where these sub-keys may exist:\par
\par
1) HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\par
\par
2) HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\ShellNoRoam\par
\par
3) HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\par
   \\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\par
\par
Using Regedit, navigate to each of these keys and determine if a sub-key called \i bags\i0  is present. Highlighting the sub-key allows a right-click menu to appear; select the \i Delete\i0  option to delete the sub-key and all data within it.  As stated above, all three potential registry areas must be checked for the \i bags\i0  sub-key; however, not all systems include all three registry keys.  All of the steps need to be followed completely as stated above and in the specified order to achieve success.\par
\par
Proof of successful operation.\par
\par
To determine if the sub-key removal and registry patch operations of \i disableautoarrange.reg\i0  were successful, open any window and click on the \ul\i View\ulnone\i0  option.  When selecting any icon-based display setting, the two options should be displayed (including the current status as indicated by a leading check mark if enabled).  In all other display settings, the two options should be displayed as greyed out.  This is totally consistent with the standard implementation available in Windows XP and Windows Vista.\par
\par
End of Appendix H.\page\f0\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f3\par
\f0 Notes and References\par
\par
\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f3\par
\f0  1. The \ul\i Poly Question Society\ulnone\i0  (abbreviated wherever possible as \ul\i P?S\ulnone\i0 ,\par
    or \ul\i PQS\ulnone\i0  if only a limited character set is available and pronounced\par
    as if spelled \ul\i pea-queue-ess\ulnone\i0 ) is an informal group of questioners\par
    concerned with a multitude of subjects.  In general, the members\par
    of the P?S (whether alive, dead or imaginary) met many years ago\par
    at an institution of higher learning known as the \ul\i Polytechnic\ulnone\par
\i0    \i  \ul Institute of Brooklyn\ulnone  \i0 or more informally \ul\i Brooklyn Poly\ulnone\i0  (now a\par
    division of \ul\i New York University\ulnone\i0 ).  Nearly all P?S members are\par
    computer programmers known for exceptional works implemented for\par
    various computer architectures, especially the \ul\i Digital Equipment\ulnone\i0\par
    \ul\i Corporation\ulnone\i0  (\i DEC\i0 ) PDP-8 family.\par
\par
    The P?S is an outgrowth of a pre-existing organization known as\par
    the \ul\i William Claude Fields Memorial Programming Group\ulnone\i0  (\ul\i WCFMPG\ulnone\i0 )\par
    (also known as \ul\i Friends of the Boston Massacre\ulnone\i0 ).  The various\par
    works of the WCFMPG are legendary in the realm of PDP-8\par
    programming; all original WCFMPG members were responsible for\par
    either starting or creating various software projects for DEC\par
    computers, especially the PDP-8.  All members of the WCFMPG were\par
    DEC employees at some point in their careers; however, much of\par
    their work for PDP-8 systems (and other computers made by other\par
    companies) predates their DEC employment.\par
\par
 2. \ul\i SIMH\ulnone\i0  stands for "history simulation" (in the sense of the history\par
    of computing) for the purpose of preserving as much as possible\par
    the functionality of computers generally no longer manufactured.\par
    The SIMH project is maintained by a loose affiliation of\par
    programmers, most generally specializing in one or two computer\par
    architectures out of the many systems supported.\par
\par
    Levels of support for specific computers varies; at one point the\par
    SIMH project was very DEC-centric but now includes support for\par
    models from various other manufacturers.\par
\par
    \f1 More information on the\f0  \f1 SIMH project can\f0  \f1 be\f0  \f1 obtained from various\par
 \f0    \f1 Internet web sites\f0  hosting components of the overall SIMH project\par
    including various source code files and other forms of\par
    documentation.\par
\par
 3. SIMH simulator support is available for additional PDP-8 features\par
    and devices normally disabled by default; however, additional\par
    options are documented within the SIMH environment by invoking\par
    certain internal \ul\i help\ulnone\i0  commands.  Where relevant, additional\par
    documentation is provided above in Appendix A.  For those\par
    \f1 seeking \f0 further\f1  \f0 information\f1 , links to\f0  certain Internet\f1  web sites\par
\f0     \f1 are provided\f0  \f1 that may supply information\f0  regarding seldom used\par
    features of SIMH; h\f1 owever, most users of PEPS will not be\par
\f0     \f1 concerned unless intending to develop software\f0  expressly t\f1 arget\f0 ing\par
\f1  \f0    specific \f1 devices.\f0\page\cf2\f3 PEPS User Guide Notes and References (Continued).\cf0\f0  \par
\par
 4. Minor adjustments may be required in certain PEPS files depending\par
    on whether the Microsoft Windows edition used is 32-bit or 64-bit.\par
\par
    Where applicable, certain third-party utilities added to the\par
    Windows system must be chosen consistently with the edition used\par
    when separate installation packages are available.\par
\par
 5. \ul\i OS/8\ulnone\i0  is the principal marketing name of an operating system that\par
    can run on most PDP-8 models.  Note: In some instances, variant\par
    systems with slightly different names were created due to the\par
    inability to support slightly incompatible hardware; the level of\par
    support provided on these systems was quite deficient and\par
    generally far less competent than most readers of this document\par
    would expect.  The design was too inflexible to support these\par
    newer systems directly; as such, incomplete and incompatible\par
    subsets were released using various marketing names.\par
\par
    This group of name-variant systems constitutes a confusing mess\par
    since some of the variations were created merely for the purposes\par
    of highly dubious marketing ploys; only an expert can tell the\par
    difference between a variant with little or no technical purpose\par
    over OS/8 Version 3D (also known as Version 3Q) and another\par
    slightly different system that can only be used with newer models\par
    such as what are known as \ul\i Omnibus\ulnone\i0  machines, or perhaps \ul\i DECmates\ulnone\i0\par
    depending on the specific variations (albeit not necessarily\par
    either complete or even completely functional).  In general it is\par
    prudent to avoid the use of these newer variants under the SIMH\par
    simulator; there is a strong likelihood of subtle (and perhaps\par
    nasty) incompatibilities appearing seemingly out of nowhere.\par
\par
    Certain specific programs acquired from newer release variants as\par
    mentioned above provide acceptable functionality when used in\par
    OS/8 V3D.  As such, the image copy of OS/8 provided in the PEPS\par
    package contains several notable programs that correct bugs and\par
    quirks in the originally released versions associated with OS/8\par
    Version V3D.\par
\par
    OS/8 was originally created as a quick-and-dirty hack pulled\par
    off over a single weekend intended as a demo program to be shown\par
    to DEC management the next Monday morning.  While there was every\par
    intention to write a second replacement system from scratch with\par
    more reasoned internal design, management foolishly insisted all\par
    further changes be made to the demo version instead of the\par
    intended complete rewrite. \par
\par
    As a direct result of this disastrous blunder, many serious\par
    structural problems became institutionalized into the design\par
    preventing correction in future work without major rewrites.\par
    (Predictably, this never happened.)\par
\par
    The original demo (and some of the follow-on work) was done by\par
    \ul\i Richard Lary\ulnone\i0 , who also created most of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0\par
    (also known later as \ul\i MS/8\ulnone\i0 ) (along with the rest of the members of\par
    the WCFMPG).  The R-L Monitor System was the first PDP-8 operating\par
    system ever used by most of the members of the P?S.\page\cf2\f3 PEPS User Guide Notes and References (Continued).\cf0\f0     \par
\par
    Part of the reason the deficient demo was created can be traced to\par
    the demonstration of the R-L Monitor System to DEC management\par
    personnel; all of them rejected the R-L Monitor System essentially\par
    because they had no idea what it was or what it actually\par
    accomplished.  All of them, at one time or another, demonstrated\par
    their profound ignorance of how a PDP-8 operating system\par
    functions, and in at least one case this directly involved the\par
    author of this document.  However, this did not stop them from\par
    making lunatic demands on how the new system should function in\par
    ways analogous to other poor operating systems, especially some\par
    superficially applied techniques as used in the \ul\i Disk Monitor\ulnone\i0\par
    \ul\i System\ulnone\i0  (which made the design somewhat worse).\par
\par
    The demo was designed to show how a computer user could approach\par
    the PDP-8 in a manner vaguely analogous to the way this was\par
    accomplished on other DEC computers available at the time (such as\par
    the use of \ul\i TOPS10\ulnone\i0  on the PDP-10 from the point of view of a time-\par
    sharing user).  As such, the managers obtained a largely\par
    superficial and flawed understanding of the demo (which for them\par
    was likely the most they could muster considering their minimal\par
    understanding of the other operating systems mentioned above\par
    available at the time).\par
\par
    It is noteworthy that all other members of the WCFMPG refused to\par
    work on the demo because of its flimsy nature.  This is especially\par
    important since their collective ideas--hashed out over the course\par
    of several months time--was the starting point for many of the\par
    features of the intended replacement system, which was now\par
    rejected out of hand by the ignorant managers.\par
\par
    Later, some other members of the P?S were hired by DEC to work on\par
    OS/8 along with a few unrelated staff members of varying\par
    abilities.  While this mixed group did eventually develop the demo\par
    into something more functional, the inherent internal limitations\par
    cannot be overcome without a complete rewrite (as was initially\par
    proposed earlier but then rejected by management).\par
\par
    Using OS/8 today, whether on real-world hardware or within the\par
    PEPS/SIMH environment, requires mastering certain unexpected\par
    skills such as working in a mythical date environment sometime\par
    prior to 01-Jan-2002.  Unfortunately, due to limitations within\par
    the file system, it is not possible to determine the proper year\par
    of any file because there is an inherent anomaly regarding which\par
    group of eight years in the range of 1970-1977, 1978-1985,\par
    1986-1993 or 1994-2001 is the true file origination date reported\par
    by the available utilities in an obfuscated manner.\page     \cf2\f3 PEPS User Guide Notes and References (Continued).\par
\cf0\f0\par
    In specific instances, certain users can ascertain the proper\par
    year grouping for particular files from personal experience.\par
    Inevitably, any collection of files will tend to become commingled\par
    with files from one of the other three possible date groups; worse\par
    still, files created today must have entirely mythical dates.  New\par
    users must keep track of the real-world date discrepancy on a file\par
    by file basis.  Because of this serious anomaly, coupled with the\par
    advantages offered by standard features of the PEPS package, it is\par
    strongly recommended all users embrace host source file editing.\par
    Within the host environment, more descriptive filenames are\par
    feasible with accurate file dates (as long as the file date can be\par
    set after 01-Jan-1980).\par
\par
    Note: Unfortunately, the prevailing host file system limitation\par
    prevents proper file date identification regarding files known to\par
    have been created between 01-Jan-1970 and 31-Dec-1977 (or possibly\par
    earlier on systems such as TOPS-10 which supports dates as early\par
    as 1964).\par
\par
    Certain intrepid users are known for manually applying pragmatic\par
    date modifications to output obtained from OS/8 directory listing\par
    utilities; this additional effort allows the maintenance of\par
    textual records beyond the capability of OS/8.\par
\par
    Note: Due to \ul\i Y2K\ulnone\i0  considerations, this practice has an upper limit\par
    of 31-Dec-2069; beyond that date, even the additional manual\par
    efforts will break down.\par
\par
 6. \ul\i P?S/8\ulnone\i0  is a replacement for the R-L Monitor System.  It includes\par
    hundreds of improvements applied to new work carried out over the\par
    course of decades.  Despite all of this effort, the basic P?S/8\par
    system runs with low overhead (and in some ways lower overhead)\par
    when compared to the original R-L Monitor System.  On certain\par
    hardware configurations, P?S/8 runs palpably faster than OS/8\par
    performing comparable tasks.\par
\par
    Certain specific concepts unique to the R-L Monitor System (and\par
    not translatable to OS/8) have been faithfully implemented\par
    in P?S/8, and in some cases actually improved.\par
\par
    P?S/8 is still currently being developed.  While the P?S/8 release\par
    included in the current PEPS package is Version 8Z (first released\par
    on 23-Feb-1988), various improvements and bug fixes have been\par
    applied to the system image included in the PEPS release.\par
\par
    When P?S/8 Version 9A is available, it will replace the present\par
    Version 8Z release and be included in future PEPS packages.\page     \cf2\f3 PEPS User Guide Notes and References (Continued).\cf0\f0\line\par
    P?S/8 runs on all of the same PDP-8 models that OS/8 (and its\par
    variants) runs on; however, a single P?S/8 release covers all\par
    models, differing only in terms of specific device handlers and\par
    related utilities where applicable.  It is noteworthy that a\par
    suitably configured P?S/8 system written to RX01 diskette media\par
    can be booted to all properly configured systems from the original\par
    "straight" PDP-8 through the DECmate II.  By way of comparison,\par
    three different OS/8 variants are required to support the same\par
    models due to insurmountable incompatibilities inherent in OS/8\par
    internal design (and at least four variants were marketed for this\par
    purpose).\par
\par
    Note: No OS/8 variant exists supporting bootable RX01 diskettes on\par
    the DECmate II.\par
\par
    A minor patch must be applied to the supplied P?S/8 release for\par
    the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller due to SIMH incompatibility with\par
    the real-world hardware; the P?S/8 keyboard monitor includes\par
    direct commands to rewind and unload DECtapes.  When applied on\par
    the real-world hardware, appropriate use of these commands helps\par
    achieve noticeable performance improvements; unfortunately, using\par
    these commands is known to destabilize the present SIMH program.\par
\par
    Documentation is available explaining the minor (reversible)\par
    patching procedure needed when moving the system image to actual\par
    DECtape hardware for use on real-world systems.  Various programs\par
    exist to move DECtape images between physical DECtapes and the\par
    SIMH-compatible files used in the PEPS environment.  Appendix D\par
    provides links to appropriate utilities for this and related\par
    purposes.\par
\par
 7. \ul\i BIN\ulnone\i0  format is the standard binary loading format defined in the\par
    \ul\i Paper-tape operating system\ulnone\i0 .  On occasion, a BIN format paper-tape\par
    image file must be loaded into memory in the SIMH simulated\par
    environment.  Both the OS/8 and P?S/8 operating systems support\par
    utilities to process BIN format data passed to the SIMH\par
    environment by setting up the \ul\i PTRIN.TXT\ulnone\i0  file with the intended\par
    binary contents.  When files are moved from the SIMH environment\par
    to the host Windows system, the \ul\i PTPOUT.TXT\ulnone\i0  file (or perhaps the\par
    \ul\i LPTOUT.TXT\ulnone\i0  file) is used for file transfer.\par
